n your workflow with Emoji Reply! 😊\n\nEffortlessly express your thoughts, appreciation, or feedback directly on text, eliminating interruptions and keeping collaboration seamless. No more breaking your concentration—just select your text, choose an emoji, and share your response instantly.\n\nBoost engagement and clarity by easily highlighting ideas or raising quick questions without cluttering the comments section. Emoji Reply keeps your workflow fun, focused, and friction-free, enhancing your productivity and team communication.\n\n### Paragraph Bookmark\n\n![Announcement - Paragraph Bookmark (1)](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/BJJCOzD7xe.jpg)\n\nSay goodbye to endless scrolling with Paragraph Bookmark. 🔖 \n\nInstantly revisit critical sections of your notes, ensuring important information is always just one click away. Whether managing detailed documentation or lengthy project plans, bookmarks streamline your workflow and make navigation effortless.\n\nStay organized and in control by adding annotations or reminders directly within bookmarked paragraphs. Keep your focus sharp and your insights accessible, simplifying collaboration and accelerating your workflow efficiency.\n\n\u003cdiv style=\"text-align: center; font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; margin: 2em 0;\"\u003e\n  \u003ca href=\"https://hackmd.io/product?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar\" target=\"_blank\" \n     style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #453aff; color: white; padding: 14px 28px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 8px;\"\u003e\n    Explore More\n  \u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\n## 🖋️ From the blog\n### How Kiwi News inspires community\n\n[Kiwi News leverages HackMD](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/06/04/kiwi-news-community?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article) to empower and engage its vibrant community. By nurturing open conversations and collaboration, they've successfully transformed their platform into a hub of innovation and shared knowledge. Their approach highlights the power of community-driven content and authentic storytelling.\n\nExplore their journey and gain insights into the strategies Kiwi News employs to foster meaningful connections, overcome collaboration hurdles, and build lasting relationships—all made seamless through HackMD's collaborative tools.\n\n\n### HackMD and web3: the perfect match \n\nLeading web3 teams are choosing HackMD to streamline documentation and enhance global collaboration. From real-time collaboration across time zones to simplifying public-facing documentation, HackMD meets the unique demands of decentralized teams, making innovation and communication effortless.\n\n[Discover how HackMD empowers web3 teams](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/09/05/web3-teams-on-HackMD?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts) by providing a unified platform that seamlessly integrates into their workflow. Accelerate your project’s growth and improve efficiency by tapping into tools explicitly designed for the dynamic and fast-paced world of web3.\n\n## 💡 Insight \u0026 Ideas\n- The Ultimate Guide to [Writing Online.](https://perell.com/essay/the-ultimate-guide-to-writing-online/)\n- CrowdStrike Delivers [Full Lifecycle Protection](https://www.stocktitan.net/news/CRWD/crowd-strike-delivers-full-lifecycle-protection-for-ll-ms-to-arhcg6t5xi75.html) for LLMs to Enterprise AI Factories with NVIDIA.\n- Expression is [Compression.](https://perell.com/essay/expression-is-compression/)\n\nThat's all this month, folks, until next time...\n\nWe can't wait to see what you create."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"57:T2b73,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cdiv class=\"author-info flex justify-start items-center\"\u003e\n    \u003cimg src=\"https://hackmd.io/_uploads/BJ3D6xK-xg.png\" class=\"img-circle\" height=\"55\" class=\"mr-4\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"ml-4\"\u003e \n        \u003cb\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://hackmd.io/@macbudkowski\"\u003eMac Budkowski\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/b\u003e\n        \u003cbr\u003e \n        Co-Founder at Kiwi News\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\n---\n\nI’m Mac Budkowski and I’m a co-founder of [Kiwi](https://news.kiwistand.com/) - a project similar to Hacker News, but focused on Ethereum. So we deliver the feed that covers close to all interesting Ethereum-related content that can be found on the Internet.\n\nIn the last 2 years, we spent a lot of time figuring out how to create an environment that makes users engaged, so here are some thoughts that I'd like to share with the [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/?nav=overview) community.\n\nWe built Kiwi because crypto moves very fast and quality content is scattered across Twitter, Telegram groupchats, Discord channels, blogs and newsletters, so it’s hard to keep up. At the same time, Hacker News has been very hostile towards crypto, so if we wanted to fix this problem, we had to build an alternative.\n\nTo ensure we don’t turn into Hacker News, from Day 1 we are open source and store all our data on top of a P2P protocol. So if someone doesn’t like our app, algorithm or moderation, they can fork us and build their own app, accessing all Kiwi links, upvotes and comments. \n\nSo far we have curated over 15,000 links and shared over 6,000 comments, and have about 1,000+ monthly users who check our app. We also have a community of a few hundreds devs, founders and people who work in Ethereum who hang out on our groupchat.\n\nRight now, Kiwi focuses on crypto tech, products, and culture, but the architecture is designed to scale across any niche that needs high-signal curation. So this is how I got into communities.\n\nWe have both the web app at https://news.kiwistand.com/\nAs well as iOS (TestFlight) app: https://testflight.apple.com/join/6jyvYECH \n\n## What community-building means to me\n\nI think real progress never starts in the main hall. It starts with 5 people eating conference lunch together, a niche subreddit or a 50‑person Telegram group grinding on the same weird problem at 3 a.m. \n\nIf we look at the history, this was the case with French impressionists, Project Manhattan, or Cypherpunks. When we put niche-obsessed people in the right environment, they move the whole domains forwards.\n\nSo building infrastructure that helps niche-obsessed people stay productive feels like a practical way to contribute to the development of Ethereum, and - in the future - other projects.\n\nI have been thinking about this subject for some time and even wrote about [“Niche Internet”](https://kanfa.macbudkowski.com/niche-internet) and [“Why niches matter?\"](https://kanfa.macbudkowski.com/why-niches-matter)\n\n## Successful community building\n\nI think it’s key to create a space where people show up not just to passively consume, but also to contribute.\n\nIn our case it’s when users actually submit and discuss links, shaping our feed. Or they give us ideas on how to improve the product. Or they spread the word about the project - either by putting our stickers on their laptops or by mentioning us in the discussions on social media. \n\nI am also a very IRL person, so for me good IRL meetups are an important indicator that something is working. Since our community is very global, we meet mostly at ETH conferences. So last year we met in Bangkok in a Korean restaurant, and an hour before the meetup I felt like everything was ready. But then it started raining. \n\nNot our European drizzle - a real, hard tropical rain that made the already tough city traffic crazy. I was worried that people wouldn't make it. But - a bit late and soaking wet - they made it and we had a great evening. \n\nWhen people put in the work to meet IRL, it’s an honest signal that they really care. \n\nFun fact, Tom Preston-Werner, GitHub Co-Founder, said that in the early days he was travelling the world and having beers with all developers he could find to build the initial community. So if IRL is so important for such a digital-first product as GitHub, it’s probably important for everyone.\n\n\n## Getting the community to engage\n\nWhen we first launched Kiwi, we had close to zero content. This meant we couldn’t create a valuable feed, and since we couldn’t create a valuable feed, we couldn’t get users. A classic chicken-egg problem.\n\nSo we started asking friends to send us 3 links they find interesting, and we put them on our website. So this was Kiwi v0.1 - just three links a day, some about crypto, some not. We called it “Editor’s Picks”. \n\nThen a user named freeatnet messaged Tim and started discussing our tech setup. Soon after - although we were a very young project with close to no users - he built his own app on top of our P2P protocol, with a different UI and algorithm.\n\nIt seemed like the way we built Kiwi - open source, very transparent - was what has drawn him, not our DAUs. Later on we received many contributions from our community. \n\nMisha started submitting great links every day, and he still does to this day. Our friend’s wife - Caroline - designed our logo and stickers. Another user - Matallo - built the first Kiwi search engine. Once he had to stop maintaining it because of other obligations, another user - Rainer - built a v2 of search engine that’s still [running.](https://knsearch.x4901.xyz/) \n\nWe had some users helping us with design, some - like Brais - printed stickers at ETH Barcelona and just gave them out to people. There was also Chris who helped us get some grants to get things going. There’s also Pugson - who runs [ENSData](https://ensdata.net/) that we use in our app - that recently donated over $1,000 to our project.\n\nAnd there are countless others who gave us feedback, supported us, and helped us remember why we do it. Being a founder is a lonely job, and since it’s just the two of us, having such a community support makes a huge difference. \n\n\n## Overcoming challenges in a community\n\nI think the biggest challenge is staying focused without killing openness. \n\nWhen you build an app like Kiwi, it’s tempting to let the community post whatever they want. It’s a community project, right? But if you're not careful, the feed quickly drifts - someone shares cat videos, it gets upvoted, and suddenly you’re Hacker News for memes.\n\nWe saw this early on and decided to actively change the course. People started asking: “Shouldn’t the community decide what content we want?” \n\nThat was a fair question but we knew that if Kiwi didn’t stay focused on high-quality crypto links, we’d lose the signal that made it valuable in the first place. So we had some tough conversations and introduced clear content guidelines. Some users left. But the ones who stayed helped make Kiwi what it is today.\n\nAlso, users always have many product ideas. If they’re active community members, they have even more. But our capacity to ship is limited, so often we have to tell people that we can’t deliver some feature or improvement they care about, and it’s very hard. \n\n## Collaborating within a community\n\nIf you look at an average online community, it’s very different from an offline one. There are people from many countries, living in different timezones, having different goals and experiences. \n\nSo there’s higher variance when it comes to people, and it’s also harder to communicate clearly because we just read text instead of having IRL conversations. That’s why we try to communicate a lot. \n\nLast time we changed the philosophy of our feed, Tim wrote a long post in our Telegram channel explaining our thinking behind it, and discussed it with the power users. \n\nWe also share our doubts and mistakes, because we believe in building in public and being transparent about our journey. \n\nWe are also both pretty straightforward, so we often discuss things that are considered taboo, which helps to strengthen the bonds between us and the users. \n\nLast time we even created this Signal group where messages disappeared after 30 seconds, because the discussed subject was hot and we wanted people to feel safe to share their takes.\n\n## How I use HackMD\n\nWe use HackMD almost all the time when we apply for grants or write bigger blog posts. Being able to just use Markdown is the feature that sold the product to me. \n\nWould be great to see HackMD become even more multiplayer-focused. At the moment we have multiplayer features for our notes - we can invite different people to share comments, run our own team and so on. \n\nAnd I think we could take it one step forward. At the moment I see people’s HackMD links only when they share them on Twitter or groupchats. But what if you ran a Discovery Feed where I could find other people’s notes based on my interest? \n\nI could follow people’s half-baked ideas, add my two cents to someone’s draft, and meet people through that channel. I think that could be really interesting.\n\n## How to strengthen your community\nCommunities don’t live in a vacuum. \n\nJust like products need to address users’ needs or otherwise will be ignored, so do communities. \n\nIf the financial system was working well, no one would be interested in Bitcoin. But it did not work well, so people had a natural need to look for an alternative. And working on Bitcoin was a way to channel your frustration into something productive. Same with Microsoft and Linux or Internet Explorer and Mozilla. \n\nSo before you start building a community, it’s good to ask yourself: what kind of problem do I want to solve for these people? Last year I did a short talk about our lessons learned, you can listen to it [here](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FwLIq-RGegM). \n\nI think it’s easy to focus on quantitative metrics here: how many people do you have in the community, how many posts do they share, how many comments do we get and so on.\n\nWhile important, I think it’s crucial to remember about qualitative metrics. Most projects would rather have a community with John Carmack than 100 junior devs. Or have 10 people sharing thoughtful, Hacker News-level comments than 100 just saying ‘hello’. Or have 3 code contributors than 30 yappers. So although the numbers are important to us, we look at the quality a lot. \n\n## What I love about HackMD\n\nThe way I look at it is that if the GitHub team wanted to create Google Docs, they’d create HackMD. So just like in Google Docs, collaboration is the foundation of this product, not an afterthought feature that’s buried somewhere deep in the UI. And the whole product has a strong hacky vibe that I really enjoy, and I think it makes it exciting for more tech-oriented communities.\n\nIf I were to name specific features, I’d say setting up teams, following people’s inputs, syncing with GitHub, and - what I particularly like - letting them [sign in via Ethereum wallets](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/26/sign-in-with-eth-is-live?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts), is great. "])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"58:T725,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe latest product update is here! 📄\n\nThis feature is part of a series of interaction boosts designed to help you more easily create content and collaborate with others. \n\n## Share how you feel with a single click\n\n![Feature update - emoji reply (1)](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/rkQbAwsbge.jpg)\n\nHave you ever wanted to share your thoughts, reactions, or encouragement without interrupting your reading flow in HackMD? With [Emoji Reply](https://hackmd.io/changelog), you can do just that.\n\nInstead of pausing to draft a response or disrupting the conversation with a new comment, react to a sentence or paragraph in the moment. Whether celebrating a great idea, showing support, or asking a quick question, Emoji Reply makes collaboration feel more natural and responsive.\n\nCommunication doesn't have to be complicated. Emoji Reply offers a lightweight and expressive way to stay connected, making it easier to engage with teammates, spark discussions, and build momentum—one reaction at a time.\n\n\n### Enhancing HackMD for you\nAt HackMD, we believe the little things make a big difference in your workflow. \n\nEmoji Reply adds that extra layer of intercation and collaboration to your workspace. It’s a fun, frictionless, lightweight way to express thoughts, appreciation, or even questions.\n\nAnd as you may have guessed, this feature is just the beginning—we’re continuing to build tools that empower you to create, edit, and collaborate with confidence and ease. \n\nWe can't wait for you to try out Emoji Reply and the upcoming features. [Log into HackMD today](https://hackmd.io/login) to see how these updates enhance your workflow. \n\nHelp shape future updates on HackMD by telling us what you'd like to see [next](https://tally.so/r/wo95EN), or hop into our [Discord](http://discord.gg/vZ9MdEw2Hu) and say hi. 💜"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"59:T7a5,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe latest product update is here! 📄\n\nThis feature is part of a series of interaction boosts designed to help you more easily create content and collaborate with others. \n\n## Bookmark and reference paragraphs in HackMD\n\n![Announcement - Paragraph Bookmark (1)](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/rk9DvC4Weg.jpg)\n\nImagine returning to the exact section of your notes that sparked your next big idea or contained that critical information without endless scrolling. With our new Paragraph Bookmark feature, you can do just that.\n\nInstead of losing precious minutes hunting through lengthy documents, quickly jump to the most important paragraphs. Keep your thoughts organized and your workflow seamless, whether you're collaborating on project plans, writing technical documentation, or drafting your next blog post.\n\nEvery note you create is unique, and so is how you work. \n\n[Paragraph Bookmarks](https://hackmd.io/@docs/paragraph_bookmark_en) allow you to highlight the most important sections, adding annotations or reminders that keep you on track and in control.\n\n\n### Enhancing HackMD for you\nAt HackMD, we believe the little things make a big difference in your workflow. \n\nParagraph Bookmark adds that extra layer of seamless collaboration, making referencing a specific paragraphs of your note easier. Your bookmarks are private and conveniently stored in a dedicated panel, where you can add annotations or jump directly to the original paragraphs.\n\nAnd as you may have guessed, this feature is just the beginning—we’re continuing to build tools that empower you to create, edit, and collaborate with confidence and ease. \n\nWe can't wait for you to try out Paragraph Bookmark and the upcoming features. [Log into HackMD today](https://hackmd.io/login) to see how these updates enhance your workflow. \n\nStay tuned for more and don't hesitate to share your thoughts with us at support@hackmd.io or in our [Discord](http://discord.gg/vZ9MdEw2Hu). 💜"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5a:Tf48,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nMay is in full swing, and we’re buzzing with fresh updates to keep your ideas flowing. 🌱\n\nAs the days grow longer and the sun shines a little brighter, we're here to help you streamline your workflow, boost productivity, and make collaboration a breeze. Whether you're gearing up for new projects or giving your workspace a little refresh, we've got you covered.\n\nReady to see what’s new at HackMD this May? Let’s dive in!\n\n--\n\n## 🌟 Visionary voices\n- [hzionn](https://hackmd.io/@hzionn) teaches you how to get Python for the first time [first time.](https://hackmd.io/@hzionn/SkrfvlP3A#Getting-Python-for-the-First-Time)\n- our very own [Yukai Huang](https://hackmd.io/@bTtGJSPIQnWijs3Cy1Ru2g) gives us a [Raycast extention](https://www.raycast.com/Yukai/hackmd) for HackMD.\n- [Alex Rickabaugh](https://hackmd.io/@alx) talks about how HackMD has helped his team at Angular in this [short video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vwULfQ3dIwA) from Tech Stack Nation.\n\n## 📄 Product updates \n\n### Version Link\n\n![Announcement - Version link](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/HkjAapGZll.jpg)\n\n\nWe’ve made it easier to reference a specific version of your note. Each saved version now comes with its own permanent link. Just click the Copy Version Link button to copy and share it instantly.\n\nWhether you’re clarifying changes with a teammate or tracking edits in a project, this feature helps you share context with confidence.\n\nRead the full update [here.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/04/28/version-link-2025?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article)\n\n\n### Image Zoom\n![Image_Zoom_2025](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/Hy5Na6MZxe.jpg)\n\n\nYour images got an upgrade! With our latest enhancement, you can click on any image or diagram to open a dedicated view, zoom in for a closer look, copy links, and even download beautifully rendered graphs like Mermaid charts. \n\nSay goodbye to squinting and screenshotting, just clean, seamless viewing, right at your fingertips.\n\n\nSee the latest enhancements in our [release notes.](https://hackmd.io/s/release-notes)\n\n\n## 🖋️ From the blog\n### Spring clean your workspace with Folders\n\nAs the season of renewal blossoms, it's the perfect time to declutter not just your home but also your digital workspace. HackMD's latest blog discusses how the Folders feature is designed to bring order to your notes and streamline collaboration. \n\nWith intuitive drag-and-drop functionality, nested folder structures, and customizable emojis, organizing your projects has [never been easier.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/03/31/what-makes-community-success?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts)\n\n\n### Privacy, ninjas and digital houses \n\n“Privacy is a public good. When I say that, I don’t mean that in a “feel good” way. I mean that in the literal sense that the more people use privacy solutions, the more privacy each one of them gets.” - [Vitalik Buterin](https://notes.ethereum.org/@vbuterin?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=web3\u0026utm_content=Vitalik)\n\nIn his insightful blog, [Mac Budkowski](https://hackmd.io/@macbudkowski) of Kiwi News delves into the nuanced realm of digital privacy, drawing parallels between our physical and online lives. He argues that while we accept a certain level of public exposure in the physical world, we cherish the sanctity of our private online workspaces, [read more here.](https://kanfa.macbudkowski.com/privacy-ninjas)\n\n## 📰 Industry news and trends\n- Ethereum [consensus shifts](https://blockworks.co/news/ethereum-consensus-evm-upgrade-fusaka-devs) on EVM upgrade.\n- A faster, sleeker [JavaScript experience.](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/nodejs-24-a-faster-sleeker-javascript-experience/)\n- Microsoft Warns of 'Ice Phishing' Threat on [Web3 and Decentralized Networks.](https://thehackernews.com/2022/02/microsoft-warns-of-ice-phishing-threat.html)\n\nUntil next time...\n\nWe can't wait to see what you create."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5b:T6ca,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe latest product update is here! 📄\n\nThis feature is part of a series of interaction boosts designed to help you more easily create content and collaborate with others. \n\n### Quickly link to any version of your note\n\n![Announcement - Version link](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/Sk87ufakel.jpg)\n\nYour new favorite way to confidently reference past work! \n\nWe've added a sleek new feature that gives every saved version of your note its own permanent link. That means with just one click, you can instantly copy and share a specific version. Perfect for clarifying edits with your team, documenting changes across a project, or keeping a clean progress record. \n\nNo more \"Wait, which version are you looking at?\" \n\n[Version Link](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fsave-version-en?utm_source=home\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar) helps you anchor your conversations in the exact moment that matters. \n\n### Enhancing HackMD for you\nAt HackMD, we believe the little things make a big difference in your workflow. Version Link adds that extra layer of transparency, making referencing a specific version of your note easier. Each saved version now comes with its permanent link. Just click the Version Link button to copy and share it instantly.\n\nAnd as you may have guessed, these features are just the beginning—we’re continuing to build tools that empower you to create, edit, and collaborate with confidence and ease. \n\nWe can't wait for you to try out Version Link and the upcoming features. [Log into HackMD today](https://hackmd.io/login) to see how these updates enhance your workflow. \n\nStay tuned for more and don't hesitate to share your thoughts with us at support@hackmd.io or in our [Discord](http://discord.gg/vZ9MdEw2Hu). 💜"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5c:T1307,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"Spring is in the air. Windows are open, plants are thriving, and everything feels fresher. While you're clearing out closets and sweeping under furniture, there's one area that often gets overlooked: your HackMD workspace.\n\nIf you've ever opened [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/?nav=overview) and been greeted by a growing list of untitled notes, rough drafts, and duplicate docs, you're not alone. It happens to the best of us. Whether juggling solo projects or working in a team, a cluttered workspace can slow down your thinking, sap your energy, and make collaboration feel like a scavenger hunt.\n\nThat's precisely why we built [Folders](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/01/13/folder-announcement-markdown?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts) — a new way to organize your notes and add some order to the chaos inside your markdown editor. Spring cleaning doesn't have to stop at home. It's time to give your workspace the refresh it deserves.\n\n\n## ⚔️ Battling digital clutter \n\nLet’s be honest: no one sets out to create a messy workspace. It just sort of... happens.\n\nOne day, you’re writing a quick outline for a meeting; the next, you’ve got twenty similar-looking docs with names like “final-v2” and “use-this-one-please.” Multiply that by a few months (or years), and your markdown editor can look more like a junk drawer than a creative space.\n\nWe get it. When inspiration strikes, the last thing you want to do is pause and figure out where your note should live. But over time, disorganization adds up. You lose time trying to find things. You lose momentum switching between projects. A collaborative markdown editor makes it easier for teammates to stay aligned.\n\n## 🏠 Your workspace, reimagined\n\nFolders is a simple but powerful way to keep your HackMD workspace neat, intentional, and easy to navigate. They help you structure your work in a way that makes sense to you and your community.\n\nHere's what you can do with folders in HackMD:\n* **Drag and drop your notes** to quickly group related content.\n* **Create nested folders** for multi-layered organization, like grouping docs by project and subtask.\n * **Personalize your folders** with emojis, adding personalized emojis to each folder is a great way to make them stand out.\n\n[Folders were designed to be intuitive](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Ffolder-en?utm_source=home\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar), not complicated. They're flexible enough to match your workflow, whether you're planning a product roadmap, managing class notes, or co-writing blog posts with community members. With a few simple moves, you can turn a scattered workspace into a streamlined system that grows with your ideas.\n\n## 📁 Organize with your community\nHackMD is more than just a markdown editor—it’s a collaborative markdown editor that brings people together to build, write, and document ideas in real time.\n\nFolders help bring clarity and consistency to community work. Whether you're managing a shared knowledge base, co-authoring documentation, or gathering feedback, folders ensure that everyone has a shared mental model of where things go and how to find them.\n\nHere are a few examples of how communities use folders to stay organized:\n\n* Open-source contributors structure notes by project or release cycle.\n* Learning communities group content by topic, week, or skill level.\n* Advocacy groups keep resources, talking points, and event docs all in one place.\n\nWith shared folders, collaboration becomes easier, faster, and more impactful. It’s organization that serves your whole community—not just the people who created the content.\n\n## 👏 Make it a habit\nA clean workspace feels fantastic, but keeping it that way is even better.\n\nSet aside time each month to review your folder structure, archive outdated content, and tidy up note titles. If your community is active and fast-moving, a lightweight system for maintenance can help prevent future clutter from piling up.\n\nYou can even build a folder template for your community to follow. For instance, a documentation folder might always include “Getting Started,” “FAQs,” “Release Notes,” and “Roadmap.”\n\nWhen your collaborative markdown editor is organized, it becomes a more powerful tool. It supports smoother workflows, encourages shared ownership, and keeps contributors focused on what matters.\n\n\n## 🌷 A Fresh start for your workspace\n\nYour HackMD workspace is where your ideas grow and growth needs breathing space.\n\nWith Folders, you can clean up digital clutter, simplify collaboration, and help your community work more efficiently. \n\nWhether leading a group, contributing to a project, or just trying to find your notes faster, a little spring cleaning goes a long way.\n\nThe more organized your workspace is, the more your community can thrive.\n\n[Start fresh today.](https://hackmd.io/home) "])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5d:Te69,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nApril is here, and we’re coming into full bloom with fresh product updates ready to grow your workflow. 🌷\n\nLike spring breathes new life into the world, this month’s updates are designed to refresh your experience, streamline collaboration, and help your ideas flourish.\n\nReady to see what’s blooming at HackMD? Let’s dive in! 🌼\n\n\n--\n\n### 🌟 Visionary voices \n- [kevinsslin](https://hackmd.io/@kevinsslin) shares an impressive presentation about his [hackathon preparation.](https://hackmd.io/@kevinsslin/BJ12ZLxhyx#/)\n- [yuna0x0](https://hackmd.io/@yuna0x0) has developed an MCP server specifically for HackMD, which allows AI agents to interact with your HackMD data and [improves your workflow.](https://github.com/yuna0x0/hackmd-mcp)\n- [elizaos](https://hackmd.io/@elizaos) has started their newsletter pipeline on HackMD allowing for an organized breakdown of current news and easy access to [all previous versions.](https://hackmd.io/@elizaos/newsletters/%2FtXineqLmT86YMAVysmCf0w)\n\n### 📄 Product updates \n\nTake a look at the latest features that were added to your workspace last week!\n\n#### Link Preview\n\n![LinkPreview_HackMD](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/BkE-Ut6CJl.jpg)\n\nLink Preview allows you to keep links as a simple URL, turn it into a clickable text link reference, generate a preview card, or—if it’s a HackMD paragraph link—cite it directly. This update makes pasting links easier, faster, and more intuitive.\n\nRead the full update [here.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/04/09/Link-Preview-Show-History?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article)\n\n\n#### Show Edit History\n![ShowEditHistory_HackMD](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/HyVM8Y6C1x.jpg)\n\nShow Edit History allows you to dive deep into the changes of specific lines without manually reviewing every version. Quickly understand the evolution of your content with a timeline of edits, including details like timestamps, editors, and modifications.\n\n\n\n\nRead the full update [here.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/04/09/Link-Preview-Show-History?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article)\n\n\n### 🖋️ From the blog\n#### What makes a community successful?\nHere at HackMD we are all about thriving communities. \n\nIn this featured blog, we explore the key ingredients behind successful communities, from shared values and clear goals to psychological safety and inclusive participation. \n\nWhether you're building a team, running a project, or growing a global network, this piece offers insights to help you foster genuine connection and [long-term impact.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/03/31/what-makes-community-success?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts)\n\n\n#### Kick-start your collaboration with Profiles\nWant to make your mark in collaborative spaces? \n\nThis month, we’re spotlighting HackMD Profiles—your personal hub for showcasing your expertise, projects, and contributions. \n\nWhether you're part of an open-source team or sharing docs across departments, Profiles help you kick-start collaboration by putting your best work front and center.\n\n[Personalize yours](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/10/25/kick-start-collaboration-profiles?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts) and get noticed in the HackMD community.\n\n### 📰 Industry news and trends\n- What would it take for ETH to [succeed?](https://blockworks.co/news/eth-data-availability-l2)\n- Microsoft Research teaches AI tools how to [debug code](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/microsoft-research-teaches-ai-tools-how-to-debug-code/)\n- The inventor of blockchain would like a [better Bitcoin](https://blockworks.co/news/bitcoin-scott-stornetta-blockchain-interview)\n\nUntil next time...\n\nWe can't wait to see what you create."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5e:Ta40,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe latest product updates are here: **Link Preview and Show Edit History!** 📄\n\nThese features are a series of interaction boosts designed to help you more easily create content and collaborate with others. \n\n#### Choose how your link appears with Link Preview\n\n![LinkPreview_HackMD](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/ByE41XVCJx.jpg)\n\nSay goodbye to clunky copy-paste formatting—HackMD’s new [Link Preview feature](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Flink_preview_en?utm_source=about\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar) makes managing links smoother than ever. \n\nWhen you paste a URL into your note, a smart selection menu pops up, letting you instantly choose how the link should appear. \n\nWhether you want to keep it as a raw URL, format it as a clickable reference, generate a sleek preview card, or cite a HackMD paragraph directly, the choice is yours. It’s a small change with big impact—it saves time, reduces friction, and keeps your notes clean and readable.\n\n\n#### Show Edit History: See specific change history\n\n![ShowEditHistory_HackMD](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/S1BGem4Cyx.png)\n\nKeep track of every change with precision thanks to HackMD’s new [Show Edit History feature](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fshow_editing_history_en?utm_source=about\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar). Instead of digging through full document versions, you can now view the edit timeline of a specific line—complete with timestamps, editor info, and exact modifications. \n\nIt’s a game-changer for collaborative writing, giving teams the transparency they need and the control they want. \n\nWhether you're reviewing updates or rolling back changes, this targeted history view makes it easy to stay aligned and efficient.\n\n#### Enhancing HackMD for you\nAt HackMD, we believe the little things add up to a big difference in your workflow. Link Preview makes pasting URLs faster and more flexible, while Show Edit History gives you line-by-line insight into how your content evolves. These updates are all about making collaboration clearer, navigation smoother, and your note-taking experience more intuitive.\n\nAnd as you may have guessed, these features are just the beginning—we’re continuing to build tools that empower you to create, edit, and collaborate with confidence and ease. \n\nWe can't wait for you to try out Link Preview, Show Edit History, as well as the upcoming features. [Log into HackMD today](https://hackmd.io/login) to see how these updates enhance your workflow. \n\nStay tuned for more and don't hesitate to share your thoughts with us at support@hackmd.io or in our [Discord](http://discord.gg/vZ9MdEw2Hu). 💜"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5f:T1e18,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nYou've seen communities come and go—some flourish while others quietly fade away. Ever stop to wonder what truly makes the difference? Successful communities don't happen by accident. \n\nThey're intentionally built around thoughtful choices, clear purpose, and authentic leadership. Let's dive into what separates the great ones from the rest and uncover insights you can immediately use in your community.\n\n## 🫂 Culture comes first \n\nCommunity culture sets the tone for everything else. For example, [The Turing Way](https://hackmd.io/@turingway?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=research\u0026utm_content=TuringWay) is an open-source guide for reproducible data science that uses HackMD to foster an inclusive, collaborative culture. They actively encourage contributors of all levels and backgrounds to co-author content, using shared docs to eliminate gatekeeping and promote transparency.\n\nLeadership author [Simon Sinek](https://simonsinek.com/) emphasizes that a strong culture is rooted in a sense of belonging and psychological safety. In [HackMD-powered communities](https://hackmd.io/solutions?utm_source=home\u0026utm_medium=solutions-nav-bar), this often looks like open invites to collaborate, clear contributor guidelines, and tools that support equal participation.\n\nPsychological safety means people feel free to share ideas, give feedback, and admit mistakes without fear. The [Rust](https://hackmd.io/@rust-lang-team?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=developers\u0026utm_content=Rust) community—another HackMD user—is famous for this. They maintain a strong code of conduct and use HackMD to collaboratively draft RFCs (Request for Comments), allowing anyone to weigh in on big decisions.\n\n## 🔋 The power of shared purpose\n\nCommunities that thrive rally around a clear, shared mission. For example, [Vitalik Buterin’s Ethereum Foundation](https://notes.ethereum.org/@vbuterin?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=web3\u0026utm_content=Vitalik) uses HackMD to develop and refine community standards and documentation for Web3 projects. Because everyone understands the broader purpose—decentralization, and innovation—it’s easier to align contributions with goals.\n\nClear purpose isn’t just about philosophy—it shows up in workflows. The [SciPy](https://hackmd.io/@scientific-python?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=developers\u0026utm_content=Sci-Py) and [Conda](https://hackmd.io/@conda-community?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=developers\u0026utm_content=Conda) communities use HackMD for shared documentation that aligns with their mission to support accessible scientific computing. With live notes and contributions, they reduce duplication, promote transparency, and build toward collective goals.\n\n\n## 💯 Leadership that empowers\n\nCommunities need leaders who enable, not control. [The CD Foundation](https://hackmd.io/@cdfoundation?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=non-profits\u0026utm_content=CDFoundation), a nonprofit that supports continuous delivery ecosystems, uses HackMD to draft open proposals and strategies. Leadership is shared—any contributor can edit, suggest, and improve the work.\n\nAuthor [Julie Zhuo](https://www.juliezhuo.com/) (\"The Making of a Manager\") notes that good leaders ask questions, listen more than they talk, and support others to take initiative. HackMD's real-time collaboration model reflects that same ethos: people can lead from anywhere in the doc.\n\nConsider [Brown University's](https://hackmd.io/@cs111?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=education\u0026utm_content=Brown) educational teams using HackMD to co-create curriculum and lesson plans. Educators and students collaborate in real-time, breaking down the hierarchy and empowering shared ownership of learning materials.\n\n## 💲Knowledge is a living asset\nCommunities thrive when knowledge isn't siloed. The [Aztec Network](https://hackmd.io/@aztec-network?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=startups\u0026utm_content=Aztec), a startup building zero-knowledge privacy protocols, uses HackMD to maintain public, editable technical documentation. It becomes a living library, refined by contributors as the tech evolves.\n\nHackMD's [version control](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fsave-version-en) and [history tracking](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fshow_editing_history_en) make every change transparent. You can always roll back or see who contributed what, which is critical for maintaining trust in collaborative environments.\n\n\n## ✨ Sustainable engagement\nCommunities like [Espresso Systems](https://hackmd.io/@EspressoSystems?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=startups\u0026utm_content=Espresso), another HackMD user, keep contributors engaged by clearly indicating how they can get involved through open doc invites, project boards, and contributor acknowledgments.\n\nProjects like [NF Core](https://hackmd.io/@nf-core?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=non-profits\u0026utm_content=NFCore) in the bioinformatics space use HackMD to document workflows and clearly credit contributors. Recognizing collaborative efforts creates a positive feedback loop that fuels continued participation.\n\n\n![How_To_Engage_2025](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/Sys9z_Oakg.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n## 🏝️ Communities as ecosystems, not islands\nCommunities don’t operate in vacuums. [Code Refinery](https://hackmd.io/@coderefinery?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=education\u0026utm_content=Code%20Refinery), an educator-led group, uses HackMD to connect students, teachers, and researchers across universities. Their documentation and collaborative training materials are openly shared and co-developed, increasing impact and innovation.\n\nHackMD also supports nonprofits like [UniCourse](https://hackmd.io/@UniCourse?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=non-profits\u0026utm_content=UniCourse) and [Marma J Foundation](https://hackmd.io/@doulos819?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=research\u0026utm_content=MarmaJ)—communities that thrive by doing good work and sharing it. Their use of HackMD makes it easier for new contributors to jump in and add value from day one.\n\n## 💪 How HackMD empowers community success\nHackMD's real-time collaborative features simplify teamwork. Members can simultaneously edit and contribute, making HackMD perfect for everything from technical documentation to event planning and collective brainstorming. Changes appear instantly across all user screens, ensuring everyone stays aligned and informed. \n\nHackMD is designed to be intuitive for all users, regardless of technical skill level. Its accessible interface ensures everyone can contribute meaningfully, supporting diverse participation and inclusivity within your community. HackMD fosters inclusivity by making participation easy and accessible for everyone. \n\nKey features include:\n* An intuitive [user interface](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Feditor_en) designed for easy adoption regardless of technical expertise.\n* [Accessible document](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Foffline-access-en) formatting to ensure readability for all members.\n* Straightforward [sharing and permissions settings](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Finvite-others-to-a-private-note-en), ensuring the right people have access.\n\n## 🔑 Success is intentional\nAt the core of every thriving community lies intentionality. Thoughtful culture, clear purpose, authentic leadership, and effective knowledge-sharing practices drive sustained engagement and success. Your community’s potential starts with your vision and deliberate actions.\n\nNow, consider this: What intentional steps will you take next to empower your community’s success? Start by creating your [HackMD community today.](https://hackmd.io/join?signup-event=HomeSignupCompleted)"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"60:Te8d,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"March is here, and luck is officially in the air—no four-leaf clovers required (though we wouldn't turn one down). \n\nAs we begin to leap into spring, we're feeling especially lucky to share exciting news of fresh partnerships, spotlight some of tech's leading voices, and unveil shiny new updates guaranteed to make your documentation feel like a pot of gold. 🌈✨\n\nReady to see what's at the end of this month's rainbow? Let's jump in! 🍀\n\n--\n\n### 🌟 Visionary voices \n- [Jin](https://hackmd.io/@xr) gives us a look into the Metaverse with his [impressive HackMD bookshelf.](https://hackmd.io/@XR/book/https%3A%2F%2Fhackmd.io%2F%40xr%2Findex)\n- [Astria](https://hackmd.io/@astriaorg) has developed a native bridging protocol for rollups built on the [Astria sequencing layer.](https://hackmd.io/@astriaorg/SJm4kyackx)\n- [Nouns Amigos](https://hackmd.io/@nounsamigos) share resources for their project and give you a [breakdown of their grants.](https://hackmd.io/C1N2BsjDR7-cWP0-EnGSeA)\n\n### 📄 Product updates \n#### Version 1.80.3 is live!\nYou may have noticed a few improvements recently within your workspace. We were excited to release the latest version of HackMD as it fixed and improved some highly requested bugs and features. \n\nThe HackMD team has been listening to YOUR feedback and working hard to make your documentation process much more manageable.\n\n[Explore the latest enhancements and improvements.](https://hackmd.io/s/release-notes)\n![Update Release Graphic](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/HydL5B13yl.png)\n\n\n\n### 🤝 HackMD x Blockfuse Labs\n\nWe're thrilled to announce our latest partnership with Blockfuse Labs, a dynamic education platform based in Nigeria that's bridging the gap between Web2 and Web3. \n\nWith Blockfuse Labs' commitment to making blockchain education accessible, inclusive, and engaging, this collaboration aligns perfectly with HackMD's passion for empowering communities through knowledge-sharing and collaborative documentation.\n\nTogether, we're opening doors for learners, creators, and innovators, fueling the future of tech education across Africa and beyond.\n\nLearn more about Blockfuse Labs here: https://blockfuselabs.com/\n\n![Partnership graphic template](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/ryKPqSk2Jx.jpg)\n\n\n### 🖋️ From the blog\n#### How I use HackMD in Open Source projects \nwe're excited to feature [Jorge O. Castro](https://hackmd.io/@castrojo), Co-Chair of Contributor Experience at Kubernetes. Jorge shares how HackMD enhances transparency and collaboration within open-source communities. \n\nHe highlights HackMD's seamless Markdown support, real-time collaboration, and effortless sharing capabilities as key tools in streamlining workflows and fostering community engagement.\n\n[Explore his insights here.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/02/26/how-i-use-hackmd-open-source?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article)\n\n\n#### A quick-start guide to community building \nNeed a comprehensive resource for transforming passive audiences into active, engaged communities? \n\n[This guide](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/02/07/community-quick-start-guide-2025?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts) walks you through defining your community's purpose, selecting the right platform, and fostering meaningful interactions. \n\n### 📰 Industry news and trends\n- MetaMask gets an overhaul with better UX, [simpler swaps](https://blockworks.co/news/metamask-ux-overhaul)\n- OpenAI launches tools to build [AI agents faster](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/openai-launches-tools-build-ai-agents-faster/)\n- ‘Short-term volatility’ [doesn’t dim crypto outlook](https://blockworks.co/news/crypto-short-term-volatility)\n\nUntil next time...\n\nWe can't wait to see what you create."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"61:T1b43,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cdiv class=\"author-info flex justify-start items-center\"\u003e\n    \u003cimg src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1264109?s=96\" class=\"img-circle\" height=\"55\" class=\"mr-4\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"ml-4\"\u003e \n        \u003cb\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://hackmd.io/@castrojo\"\u003eJorge O. Castro\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/b\u003e\n        \u003cbr\u003e \n        Co-Chair Contributor Experience at Kubernetes\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\n---\n\nOne of the major properties of successful open source projects is their ability to be transparent with their communities. \nThis involves everything from open governance, to values, to technical direction. \n\nIn the past, OSS projects didn't have many options for tooling. The organized ones ran their own mailing lists and contributors to those projects seemingly spent as much time on their project infrastructure as they did on the projects themselves. As SaaS became more prevalent, the amount of options grew exponentially, but so did the complexity and choice overload. \n\nTherefore when a simple, powertool comes along it can make an impression. \n\nOne of the things I've been doing for a few years is running a [Kubernetes Office Hours](http://k8s.dev/events/office-hours). I also participate in [TGIK](https://tanzu.vmware.com/developer/tv/tgik/), a livestream, and many of [VMware's Open Source projects](https://www.vmware.com/opensource.html). All of these require taking notes, working with a community of contributors, and then committing the notes somewhere for future reference. \n\nHere's how I ended up using HackMD across the board:\n\n### Markdown\n\nMarkdown is the lingua franca of OSS now. We write our READMEs in it. \nWe write our docs with it and use wonderful tools like [hugo](https://gohugo.io/) to export really nice looking [documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/) and [websites](https://kubernetes.io/). Any tool I use needs to have this support built in. \n\n - I can import/export anything to our GitHub repo and it just works.\n - I can import/export anything to our [Discourse forums](https://discuss.kubernetes.io/) and it just works. \n - I can prototype documentation with a group of people and do all the commenting, review, and nitty gritty work in one document.\n\nI used to prototype something in a Google Doc then send it to a list. Wait for a bunch of people to ask me for the right permissions, finally get all the reviews done, and then convert it to Markdown for a pull request. \n\nGoogle docs are great for writing documents, but for technical content that will end up living in a repo, nothing beats native markdown and a git repository. Having native GitHub authentication makes this easy since people use the same accounts they use when reviewing the final content. \n\n### It's easy for people to join in\n\nOur [TGIK podcast](https://tanzu.vmware.com/developer/tv/tgik/) is a YouTube livestream with a live audience. \nWe use custom DNS so that our show notes are always at `tgik.io/notes`, this makes it easy and rememberable. Every episode we ask people to dive into the notes while the show is happening. YouTube's chat functionality doesn't allow for people to paste in links, and even if it did, there's something to be said for making the audience put the show notes and URLs for things they find interesting in the notes directly. \n\nIt cleans up the chat and gives our audience a sense of ownership around the topic we're discussing. The host prepares the notes ahead of time, and the audience fills in the gaps as the host is doing the demo and the actual show. This has also allowed us to build an archive as we went along which we can easily push to a git repository. \n\n### It's fast\n\nNot much to say here. :D \n\n### Anyone can use it (or not use it)\n\nEveryone in the projects I work on use a GitHub account. It also supports other popular account methods. However a surprising feature is how it works when no one signs in at all. If they don't want to sign in they don't have to, I can make accounts that enforce a sign in or not, the option is up to me. \n\nHere's how it works as an example:\n\n- I come up with a great idea, I immediately fire up a new page and start writing. \n- I can send the link to the people I want to see it, and they can begin writing immediately. \n- We decide if the idea is good I then quickly check the permissions to ensure they are correct and then share with a wider group.\n\n_The initial set of people I sent it to can begin writing immediately with zero friction_. \n\nWhen I mention \"It's fast\" I don't mean just the software itself. I mean the workflow. \n\nSimilarly, people who don't use hackmd don't need to see it. \n\nSince it's markdown-native you can happily just see the raw text when I PR it. You can review it in GitHub, you don't need to care about the dozens of people who might have contributed to it, or all the comments I have addressed while drafting. I can do all of those things ahead of time before submitting a review. \n\n- Is my submission too unfinished? \n\nEasy, I close it and give you the hackmd link, then we go into deep review with comments, rewrites, and hardcore dissection. I can easily spit out another PR. It's all just Markdown. \n\n### Sharing is easy\n\nI can click one button and share the URL almost immediately. No digging into menus and no \"Create link of this document?\" to hide some unweildly URL. I share immediately and there's 2 simple dropsdowns for permissions. I default to open for everything since when I'm drafting I want to be able to send a link to folks and have them dive right in.\n\n![](https://i.imgur.com/gS3aVyZ.png)\n\nNotice the custom field for the share name, I don't need to use a URL shortener either, one less thing for me to worry about.\n\nI can easily change the permissions at any time quickly via the share button. \n\n### Templates\n\nTemplating by itself is a simple feature. But if you haven't guessed by now there's lots of value in being able to share my notes templates with other projects. \n\nI can reuse them in GitHub or Discourse, document them on my hugo page, and keep them all consistent. Again, by choosing Markdown as the language I can easily share across all my properties. \n\n\n### Exporting and Self Hosting\n\nThe usual basics are there, however, I want to talk about exporting in a more strategic manner than just \"It can export to Dropbox\". \n\nThe ability to self host via [CodiMD](https://github.com/hackmdio/codimd) is crucial.\n\nI don't use this, part of the value for me is not being a system administrator, but having this as an option for folks is becoming rarer these days, but it is a crucial value that should be lauded. We also don't host our own Discourse forums, but the root value of having self-hosted options is a nice guarantee from software that we use every day. \n\n### Conclusion\n\nThere are a bunch of [features](https://hackmd.io/features) I haven't even talked about yet (like using it for [slidedecks](https://hackmd.io/slide-example)). I use them on occasion but for my usage it's all about the core workflow: Create, share, and then publish/commit."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"62:T15c2,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"January felt like it was never going to end. Was there an extra week in there? But we made it! As we stumble into February, love is in the air, not just the sappy, heart-shaped kind. \n\nWe're talking about a passionate romance with documentation. \n\nNothing says \"commit\" (no, I don't mean GitHub) like a well-organized note, right?\n\nThis month, in addition to featuring the latest industry news, informative blogs, and community voices, we're giving you an exclusive interview with our CEO and CTO and some new updates you may have missed.\n\nLet's dive right in...\n\n--\n\n### 🌟 Visionary voices \n- Emmanuel gives a comprehensive, concise overview of peerDAS, explaining why it is necessary and how it functions, [all in one place.](https://hackmd.io/@manunalepa/peerDAS/https%3A%2F%2Fhackmd.io%2F%40manunalepa%2FB1idHCOfke)\n- Cedoor talks bridging cryptography between protocols in [from silos to synergy.](https://hackmd.io/@cedoor/HJ-pyQ7ryl)\n- Choz highlights their project well by showing us what can be done with their [highly customized note.](https://hackmd.io/@choz/H1A08kyrkg?type=view)\n\n### 📄 Product updates \n#### Folders is live!\nIn mid-January, we introduced Folders, a highly anticipated feature that makes organizing your HackMD notes easier than ever. \n\nFrom managing a growing collection of technical documentation, brainstorming ideas, or collaborating with your team, Folders provide a simple yet powerful way to structure your workspace. \n\nNo more sifting through endless notes—now you can categorize, navigate, and access your content effortlessly. If you haven't tried it, now is the perfect time to experience a more streamlined workflow. \n\n[Learn more about Folders.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/01/13/folder-announcement-markdown?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts)\n\n![folders_create](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/SkCJf-YFyx.gif)\n\n\n### 🎙️ Commons Economy Roadmap Interview\n\nWe are thrilled to share an interview featuring our CEO, Jong-Kai Yang, and CTO, Max Wu, conducted by the Commons Economy Roadmap (CER). CER is a knowledge base and promotion protocol highlighting and empowering leading projects dedicated to building open infrastructure for societal regeneration. \n\nIn this discussion, Jong-Kai and Max delve into the evolution of collaborative knowledge-building tools and explore the innovative features that set our platform apart. Their conversation offers a deep dive into our mission and the future of collaborative workspaces. Don't miss this opportunity to look at our journey and vision behind the scenes.\n\nLearn more about the Commons Economy Roadmap here: https://info.commonseconomy.org/\n\u003ciframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/DcLotJZb4xw?si=X2g7UYVsclz-FBgx\" title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen\u003e\u003c/iframe\u003e\n\n### 🖋️ From the blog\n#### HackMD \u0026 Obsidian: The ultimate markdown workflow \nFor those who love the power of Obsidian but need seamless collaboration, our blog explores how HackMD and Obsidian can work together to create the ultimate knowledge management workflow. \n\nIt doesn't matter if you're an individual note-taker or part of a team; this guide explains how to integrate both platforms to maximize local markdown editing and real-time collaboration. \n\nIf you're looking to streamline your workflow without compromising flexibility, this is a [must-read.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/01/22/hackmd-obsidian-workflow?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts)\n\n\n#### What is is community building and why does it matter? \nHackMD is evolving beyond just a markdown editor into a space where ideas thrive. \n\nFrom open-source contributors to remote teams, we’re fostering a dynamic ecosystem where users can connect, create, and grow together. \n\nOur recent blog dives into why it matters now more than ever... [Read the full blog here.](https://hackmd.io/blog/2025/01/30/community-building-hackmd-2025?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article)\n\n### 📰 Industry news and trends\n- How crypto markets are handling [tariff updates.](https://blockworks.co/news/crypto-markets-tariff-reaction) \n- Best practices for CI/CD migration: [The GitHub Enterprise example.](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/best-practices-ci-cd-migration-github-enterprise-example/)\n- Solana: [A beginner-level explainer.](https://blockworks.co/news/solana-beginner-level-explainer)\n- The role of AI in [streamlining workspace](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/the-role-of-ai-in-streamlining-workspace-management-processes/) management processes.\n- Web3 growth will be [synonymous](https://cointelegraph.com/news/web3-growth-and-ethereum-growth) with Ethereum growth.\n- [Only 4 percent of jobs rely heavily on AI](https://www.theregister.com/2025/02/11/ai_impact_hits_midtohigh_wage_jobs/), with peak use in mid-wage roles.\n- Blockchain technology needs to become [invisible to go mainstream.](https://cointelegraph.com/news/blockchain-technology-needs-to-become-invisible)\n- Vance warns against 'tightening the screws' [on AI in rebuff of Europe.](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/jd-vance/vance-ai-regulation-europe-criticizes-rcna191482)\n\nDo you have an article or paper you'd like featured in the Markdown Memo? We'd love to hear from you! [Tag us on Twitter](https://twitter.com/hackmdio) or [share it on reddit.](https://www.reddit.com/r/HackMD/)\n\nUntil next time...\n\nWe can't wait to see what you create."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"63:T25c0,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nSo you've built a following...\n\nIt may start with blog posts, open-source contributions, or technical documentation that resonates with people in your industry. Your content gets traction, likes, comments, and even discussions in DMs or GitHub issues. But **engagement isn't the same as community**. \n\nWhat if you could turn those passive interactions into a living, breathing collaboration ecosystem?\n\nThis guide will walk you through building a community from the ground up. Whether you're looking to share knowledge more effectively, or grow an open-source project, this is your quick-start roadmap to creating a self-sustaining community.\n\n\n## 🔎 Define your purpose \n\nBefore building, step back and define why your community should exist. A strong community has a clear purpose that aligns with its members' needs. You aim to create a space that fosters meaningful interactions and delivers value beyond what people can get from simply following your content.\n\nAsk yourself key questions:\n1. **What common interests or goals unite my audience?** \n2. **What problems can a community help solve that my content cannot?** \n3. **How does this community fit into my brand, project, or business?** \n\nThese answers will shape the foundation of your community and help guide future decisions on engagement and growth.\n\nOnce you define the purpose, communicate it. A **well-articulated mission statement** helps set expectations and attracts the right members. Your community should feel like a place where people genuinely benefit from participating, whether through learning, collaboration, or networking.\n\n\n\n## 🏠 A place for your community to live\n\nYour community needs a home: a dedicated space where members can connect, collaborate, and engage in meaningful discussions beyond just comments or DMs. The right platform depends on your goals, audience, and the interactions you want to facilitate. Here are some of the most popular options:\n- [Discord](h[ttps://](https://discord.gg/eSmUw8hX)) – Ideal for real-time conversations, casual interactions, and voice discussions. It’s widely used for developer communities, open-source projects, and Web3 groups due to its flexibility and channel-based structure.\n- [X (formerly Twitter)](https://x.com/hackmdio) – Known for its growing Communities feature, this social platform offers discussion threads, short-form content sharing, and real-time updates. It is ideal for fostering niche groups and engaging audiences beyond traditional social feeds.\n- [Reddit](https://www.reddit.com/r/HackMD/) – Best for communities that thrive on discussions, AMAs, and crowdsourced knowledge. Subreddits provide a structured way to facilitate Q\u0026A, share insights, and engage in topic-driven conversations.\n\nIt is important to start with one platform that best aligns with your goals. Choosing the right home from the beginning helps set the stage for deeper interactions and long-term sustainability.\n\n## 🪴 Growing your community on HackMD\nHackMD distinguishes itself by prioritizing thoughtful, accountable discussions over rapid interactions common in most online platforms. This is appealing to community builders who value depth and long-form discussions.\n\nOur platform supports collaborative content portability with the Markdown format, allowing users to retain ownership of their work and avoid the lock-in effect others impose. It also enables participation without needing an account, removing barriers for casual observers to contribute valuable insights. \n\n### How HackMD complements other tools\nHackMD isn’t meant to replace existing community tools; it enhances and complements them by enabling:\n- **Contextual, in-depth discussion** – Features like [Suggest Edit](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fsuggest-edit-en), [Comments](https://hackmd.io/s/how-to-use-comments), and [Version History](https://hackmd.io/s/how-to-save) ensure discussions remain focused, structured, and transparent rather than chaotic and fragmented.\n- **Multi-modal engagement** – HackMD allows users to embed images, code snippets, [UML diagrams](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/21/what-is-a-uml-diagram?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts), LaTeX equations, and even Figma frames, making discussions more dynamic and visually enriched.\n- **Structured community documentation** – Unlike chat-based platforms where important information gets lost in endless scrolling, HackMD helps communities organize and present key knowledge clearly—perfect for codes of Conduct, meeting minutes, and contribution guides.\n- **Seamless integration with GitHub and other platforms** – HackMD is a natural fit for communities that rely on [GitHub for collaboration](https://hackmd.io/s/link-with-github), ensuring smooth workflows and content versioning.\n- **A bridge between synchronous and asynchronous collaboration** – While Discord and Slack excel at real-time conversations, HackMD provides a structured space for long-form, reflective discussions and documentation that communities can reference over time.\n\n\n## 💬 Build a culture, not just a chatroom\n\nA thriving community isn’t just a collection of people; it’s a culture. Establishing guidelines for respectful discussions, inclusivity, and collaboration is key. Define clear roles for moderators or ambassadors who can help foster a positive environment. Encourage members to take ownership of discussions and contribute meaningful content.\n\nSome of the best technical communities have built strong cultures by clearly defining their values and making it easy for members to contribute. \n\nHere are a few examples of successful communities:\n\n[The Rust Community](https://hackmd.io/@rust-lang-team?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=developers\u0026utm_content=Rust) fosters a welcoming environment through its well-documented Code of Conduct and structured working groups that encourage collaboration across different areas of development.\n\n[The Python Community](https://hackmd.io/@scientific-python?utm_source=solutions\u0026utm_medium=developers\u0026utm_content=Sci-Py) centers on inclusivity and learning. The Python Community Page highlights various engagement ways, including mentorship programs, mailing lists, and local meetups.\n\n[The Kubernetes Community](https://hackmd.io/@k8s) has a well-structured contributor guide and active mentorship programs to help onboard new contributors and ensure a strong, knowledgeable member base.\n\n---\n\nYou can model your community-building efforts after what has worked for others in the industry. Create clear guidelines, facilitate mentorship, and provide accessible entry points for new members to engage and contribute.\n\n![community_building_quote](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/Hy3xUl5qJx.png)\n\n\n## 🎨 The art of engagement\n\nBuilding a community is more than just having a platform; it’s about fostering active participation. Engage members by making them feel involved and providing reasons for them to contribute. Start with simple activities like asking questions, running polls, sharing insights.\n\nCreate structured opportunities for interaction, such as weekly discussion threads, AMAs, and collaboration projects. Encourage members to share their work and experiences to promote a sense of ownership.\n\nAcknowledge contributions by recognizing member participation through shoutouts, featuring their work, or offering small incentives. \n\nAs your community grows, establish recurring ways to connect that members anticipate, such as monthly meetups or collaborative sessions. This will transition your community from passive consumption to an engaged, self-sustaining network.\n\n## 📈 Engagement over followers\nMany founders make the mistake of measuring success by the number of followers rather than the quality of engagement. But a large, inactive community is far less valuable than a small, deeply engaged one. Instead of focusing on growth, track the right signals.\n### Are members coming back multiple times?\nA one-time visitor may be curious, but repeat engagement signals that they find ongoing value in the community.\n### Are discussions moving beyond surface-level comments?\nA thriving community fosters substantive conversations where members actively exchange ideas, challenge perspectives, and build upon each other's thoughts.\n### Are members creating their own discussions or sharing knowledge?\nA healthy community isn’t just reactive; it’s proactive, with members taking ownership of discussions.\n\nIf engagement starts to feel stagnant, ask yourself:\n\n- *Is the conversation too broad?* (Narrowing focus can deepen engagement.)\n- *Are we attracting the right people?* (Refining outreach strategies can help.)\n- *Are we making it easy for people to participate?* (Lowering the barrier to contribution can improve interaction.)\n\n💡 **Tip:** Adapt and iterate based on engagement signals. A community is built through continuous listening and adjustment.\n\n## 👣 Taking your first steps\n\nBuilding a community may seem overwhelming at first, but the most important step is simply getting started. Define your purpose, choose a platform, and start engaging with your audience. Keep your focus on creating value, encouraging conversations, and fostering a welcoming space where members feel empowered to participate.\n\nThe best communities grow organically, fueled by shared interests and meaningful interactions. Your role as the community leader is to guide, facilitate, and provide the right environment for members to thrive.\n\nSo, what’s stopping you? [Take that first step today](https://hackmd.io/join?signup-event=HomeSignupCompleted) and start turning your audience into a community.\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"64:T18b9,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nCommunity is the heart of human connection. \n\nBuilding a community in a world increasingly defined by digital interactions will be crucial in 2025, as it fosters a sense of belonging and collaboration and unlocks the power of shared knowledge and collective goals. \n\nThat is why at [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/home), we’re not just about markdown editing; we’re about creating vibrant spaces where you can connect, share, and grow with like-minded individuals.\n\nToday, we are going to dive into what community building means for you and why it’s so important as technology increasingly takes over our lives.\n\n## What is community building?\nCommunity building at its core is the idea of bringing people together into a shared and safe space, whether in person or online, where they can connect over similar goals, interests, or values. It’s not just about assembling a group; it’s about creating meaningful relationships that encourage collaboration and mutual support.\n\nThink about the communities you’re already part of. Maybe it’s your neighborhood group, a professional network, or an online platform where you exchange ideas. Each space thrives when members contribute, communicate, and work together to achieve something bigger than themselves.\n\nCommunity building revolves around a few key elements. First, a shared purpose is a common interest or goal that connects everyone involved. Then there’s engagement, each member actively participating and contributing to the group’s success. Trust is also crucial, as is fostering respect and reliability within the group. Collaboration brings it all together, offering opportunities for members to work as a team toward shared outcomes.\n\n\n\n## Why does community building matter?\n\n### For you personally:\nBeing part of a community gives you a sense of belonging. It's a space for support, encouragement, and inspiration. Communities provide opportunities to share your ideas, learn from others, and grow as an individual. Whether joining a local group or collaborating online, connecting with others helps you feel valued and understood, boosting your confidence and overall well-being.\n\n###  For your career:\nCommunities are a catalyst for your professional growth. They spark innovation by bringing together people with diverse perspectives and ideas. You can network with like-minded professionals, find mentors, and discover collaborative opportunities that can open doors to new possibilities. Being part of a community also helps you stay informed about industry trends and gives you access to resources that can enhance your skills and career trajectory.\n\n## Community building on a larger scale\n\nLet's say you already have a larger project or broader audience. You may be asking yourself how this can benefit you and your followers?\n\nCommunity building is essential for connecting your audience and projects. In today's digital-first world, online communities give you a direct line to the people who matter most, whether they are your team, supporters, or collaborators. Unlike platforms driven by algorithms, building a community is about creating an owned space where real connections happen and conversations flow freely.\n\nAn engaged community helps you get valuable feedback to shape your project, ensuring it aligns with the needs of the people it serves. It becomes a two-way relationship: you provide value, and your community shares insights, ideas, and support. This can lead to more trust, stronger loyalty, and ultimately better results for what you're working on. Communities are also powerful growth engines. \n\nCommunity-building activities can attract new collaborators, spark innovative ideas, and even open doors to opportunities you might not have discovered otherwise. Whether you're launching a creative project, growing a team, or trying to make a bigger impact, community-building activities ensure that your work is supported by people who care and are ready to grow alongside you.\n\n![community quote](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/ry72mC_dkl.png)\n\n## Technology for successful community building\n\nTechnology is vital in building and maintaining communities, especially in the digital age. Large digital platforms allow people to connect, collaborate, and share ideas regardless of geographic barriers. By leveraging the right tools, you can expand your reach and build meaningful connections within any community.\n\nHere are some examples of how digital platforms foster community building:\n\n- [**GitHub:**](https://hackmd.io/s/link-with-github) Developers collaborate on open-source projects, sharing expertise and improving software worldwide.\n- [**Reddit:**](https://www.reddit.com/r/HackMD/) Subreddits create smaller, focused communities where people connect over shared interests, whether hobbies, career advice, or niche discussions.\n- [**Discord:**](https://discord.gg/f8QJczkg) Channels enable real-time communication and collaboration. It is often used for gaming, study groups, and interest-based communities.\n\nIn addition to these platforms, advanced technologies like artificial intelligence (AI) and augmented reality (AR) are revolutionizing how communities interact. AI can match you with like-minded individuals or suggest communities that align with your interests, making it easier to find your tribe. AR tools enhance engagement by creating immersive experiences, helping people connect in more personal and interactive ways.\n\nBy using these technologies effectively, you can create an inclusive, engaging, and impactful thriving community. The key is to choose tools that align with your goals and encourage active participation among members.\n\n## The beginning of your community\n\nCommunity building creates meaningful connections that lead to shared growth and innovation. Whether through digital platforms, local networks, or global collaborations, communities thrive when people come together with purpose and engage in ways that matter. \n\nTechnology has made starting and sustaining these connections easier, offering tools that empower you to build communities that align with your goals and passions.\n\nAre you ready to build your community? This is only the beginning.\n\n[Find a space](https://hackmd.io/join?signup-event=HomeSignupCompleted) where you can connect, collaborate, and grow with others today."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"65:T1f1b,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nMarkdown tools have simplified and streamlined how you take notes, draft documents, and organize information. However, no single tool can meet every need regarding collaboration, personal knowledge management, and workflow efficiency.\n\nSo today, we're kicking off a series highlighting how [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/home) can be used within different workflows. Starting with a Markdown tool you might be familiar with: [Obsidian.](https://obsidian.md/)\n\nCombining HackMD’s collaborative features with Obsidian’s organizational tools, you can unlock a seamless and highly productive markdown workflow tailored to you. This workflow includes collaborative document creation for teams, project planning and organization for communities, and integration of long-term knowledge management into personal workflows.\n\nLet’s dive into what makes these tools unique and how they can work together to transform your workflow.\n\n## **What is HackMD?**\n\n### **A markdown tool for seamless collaboration**\n\nHackMD is a powerful markdown editor that enables you to work together in real-time with your team and wider community (think Google Docs but for developers). Its intuitive interface makes collaboration simple and efficient, allowing you to easily co-create documents.\n\nLeave comments asking for clarification, suggesting edits to wording, or tagging teammates to add their expertise. Whether you're drafting meeting notes, technical documentation, or presentations, HackMD provides the perfect platform for seamless teamwork. That's what HackMD was built for: collaboration.\n\nWith privacy and control features like private workspaces and individual access settings, you can rest easy knowing your content is secure while maintaining flexibility in your teamwork.\n\n### **The perfect companion for building community**\n\nHackMD empowers teams and projects to share and develop knowledge not only for their community *but with them, as well*.\n\nEducation groups can use it to collaborate on lesson plans or research summaries published for students or fellow researchers. Online collectives rely on its collaborative features to document and share their whitepapers, community guidelines, and FAQs.\n\nPublic sharing links and the ability to invite feedback make HackMD a key tool for community-driven initiatives.\n\n### **Integrations and Book Mode**\n\nHackMD integrates with [GitHub](https://hackmd.io/s/link-with-github) to allow developers to manage documentation alongside their codebase. Its [Sign-in With ETH](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/26/sign-in-with-eth-is-live) feature ensures secure access for decentralized projects, while integration with [Arweave](https://hackmd.io/@docs/export-import-arweave-en) offers permanent, tamper-proof document storage for critical information.\n\nAlong with robust integration, you can harness [Book Mode](https://hackmd.io/s/how-to-create-book) to transform individual markdown documents into a cohesive, structured format. This feature is particularly useful for creating tutorials, wikis, and training manuals that require easy navigation and readability.\n\n## **What is Obsidian?**\n\n### **A markdown tool for personal knowledge**\n\nObsidian is a tool designed to manage personal knowledge using markdown. With Obsidian, you can organize your thoughts and create personal repositories of knowledge that are easy to update and reference.\n\nObsidian's graph view provides a bird's-eye perspective of your notes, helping you visualize relationships and explore new insights into your ideas. Its bidirectional linking system allows you to connect related notes easily and provides another way to explore the relationship between concepts.\n\nWhether you’re managing a research project, planning a novel, or organizing tasks, the ability to link concepts dynamically helps uncover deeper relationships within your work.\n\n### **Plugins and themes for customization**\n\nAlong with its intricate organizational capabilities, Obsidian’s robust plugin ecosystem allows users to extend its core functionality with tools for task management, habit tracking, and more. Community-created themes also allow users to tailor the app’s appearance to fit individual preferences.\n\n## **Bringing them together as one**\n\n### **Combine HackMD’s collaboration with Obsidian’s personal knowledge management**\n\nIf you prefer to use Obsidian for your personal docs, integrating HackMD and Obsidian can bridge the gap between collaboration and personal productivity. \n\nFor example, say you are working on your white paper in Obsidian. Before sharing this idea with your community for feedback, you can use this document to refine your thoughts. \n\nOnce you're ready, export it to HackMD, where members can collaborate further by making comments, adding edits, and bookmarking it so they can review it later.\n\n### **A workflow to boost productivity and enhance project management**\n\n1. **Kickoff and Collaboration in HackMD:** Begin projects in HackMD by drafting initial ideas collaboratively. [Invite team members](https://hackmd.io/s/invite) to provide real-time input through shareable links and use suggestion and commenting features to refine the document collaboratively.  \n   * *Example:* A team working on a product launch uses HackMD to create a marketing strategy. Each member contributes ideas for social media campaigns, ad copy, and timelines, which are consolidated into a single, cohesive plan.  \n2. **Finalization and Integration in Obsidian:** Once collaboration is complete, export the polished document to Obsidian. Use Obsidian’s bidirectional linking to connect the finalized plan to related notes, such as competitor analysis, customer research, or past campaign results.  \n   * *Example:* The finalized marketing plan is linked to notes on brand positioning and audience demographics, creating a centralized resource for future campaigns.  \n3. **Iterate and Expand:** Return to HackMD for updates or further collaborations. Sync these changes back to Obsidian to ensure your knowledge base remains accurate and comprehensive.  \n   * *Example:* After executing the campaign, the team documents the results and lessons learned in HackMD. The updated file is then synced to Obsidian and linked to the original plan for easy reference.\n\n### **Strategies to sync your notes seamlessly**\n\nMaintaining synchronization between HackMD and Obsidian can be achieved through various methods:\n\n1. **Markdown exports and imports**: You can [import documents](https://hackmd.io/@docs/export-and-import-notes-en) from Obsidian in a markdown format straight into HackMD. This manual method is straightforward and ensures all content is preserved.  \n2. **Cloud storage integration**: Use services like Dropbox, Google Drive, or OneDrive to maintain shared folders that sync automatically. By storing markdown files in these shared locations, you can access updates made in HackMD directly from Obsidian.  \n3. **Third-party tools**: Utilize automation tools or custom scripts to set up workflows that automatically sync content between HackMD and Obsidian.\n\nBy combining HackMD’s real-time collaboration with Obsidian’s robust organizational features, you can create a workflow that boosts team productivity and supports long-term knowledge retention and management throughout your community.\n\n## **Moving Forward**\n\nThe combination of HackMD and Obsidian offers a unique approach to managing collaborative and personal productivity.\n\nHackMD enables teams to brainstorm, draft, and refine content in real-time, while Obsidian provides a structured and organized space to store and link that knowledge for long-term use.\n\nIntegrating these tools can significantly elevate your workflow, whether you are part of a fast-paced team or an individual looking to enhance your note-taking and project management.\n\n[Get started today](https://hackmd.io/join?signup-event=HomeSignupCompleted) to embrace the combined power of HackMD and Obsidian."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"66:Te99,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nOrganizing notes in [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/home) just got a serious upgrade. Today we're delighted to introduce you to Folders -- a new way to organize and structure your workspace for easy navigation and management.\n\nFrom day one, HackMD has built features to help both teams and individuals collaborate. Whether you're juggling meeting notes, brainstorming ideas, or working on your next big project, Folders makes it easier than ever to keep everything streamlined and organized. \n\n#### A cleaner, smarter workspace\nGone are the days of a long, scrolling list of notes. Now you can group related documents together (like projects, timeframes, or categories) for a cleaner, clutter-free workspace. \n\nAnd tags can seamlessly integrate into your folder workflow. Use folders for broad categories (like \"Team Resources\" or \"2025 Events\") and tags for more specific attributes (like \"Design Doc,\" \"Meetings Notes,\" or \"Code Review\"). This hybrid approach gives you multiple ways to categorize and find what you need, no matter how many documents you're managing. \n\nSo say you're part of a technical team using HackMD to manage product development. With Folders, you could organize your workspace like this:\n1. Create folders by project\n2. Add tags to specify different types of docs \n\nThen, when it's time for your weekly check-in, use the full-text search function enabled with [your Prime account](https://hackmd.io/join?create-paid-team=true) to pull up all notes tagged with \"Meeting Notes\" across any folder. Or, drill down into the project folder for a focused view of work in progress. \n\nBut you might be asking, how does Folders differ from Book Mode, a feature already available within HackMD? While both are used to organize and group notes, Book Mode allows you to easily group and view multiple notes _within one document_. It can be viewed both internally and externally, depending on permissions. The [HackMD Tutorial](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials) is a good example of Book Mode in action. While with Folders, if you want to organize notes together, they don't _have_ to live in Book Mode. You now have multiple options for organizing your content. \n\nBetween tags, Book Mode, and Folders, you can keep the big picture clear, while still ensuring your team has quick access to the details. \n\n#### Batch actions and personalization\nReady to tidy up your space? Select multiple notes at once for batch actions, like moving docs into a folder. We know you've written quite a few notes and organizing them now might feel a little overwhelming. That's why we made sure to include the batch action functionality. So you can organize quickly and get back to building. \n\nOnce your notes are organized into folders, take it a step further by adding a little flair. Drag and drop folders in order of priority or customize folder icons with emojis to make your workspace feel uniquely you -- all with the added bonus of making it even easier to find what you're looking for.\n\n![upload_dd4dd307a8e51e247cf1b39ffa438694](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/r1XH217PJg.jpg)\n\n#### Enhancing your HackMD experience\nWe're obsessed with building you the best tool for your collaboration, code building, and knowledge sharing. And this feature isn't just about aesthetics -- it's about delivering one of the most requested features for you. Folders are here to simplify your workflow, save you time, and ultimately help you build the next great thing.\n\nStart organizing your workspace with Folders today and experience a cleaner, smarter way to collaborate. Once you've created your new workspace, show it off! Snap a screenshot of your beautifully organized folders, share it on social media, and tag us. \n\nWe can't wait to see what you create! \n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"67:T1381,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nCan you believe it? Just like that 2024 is over and now it's time to set our sights on 2025! \n\nThis month, along with featuring the latest industry news, informative blogs, and community voices, we're announcing our Holiday Giveaway winner and some new features you may have missed.\n\nLet's get to it...\n\n--\n\n### 🌟 Visionary voices \n- kevaundray 