you have that set and aligned internally, you could publish your note, sharing it with your wider community for feedback and visibility. By sharing with your community, you're now fostering a community built on transparency and transparency leads to helpful feedback, further refining your roadmap and strategy.\n\n### The power of shared goals in 2025\n\nDo you know what’s better than setting goals? Crushing them with your team and sharing them with your community. Create a [shared space where everyone can connect](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/11/07/Copy-Link-Floating-Toolbar), collaborate, and celebrate together. Here are a few ideas:\n\n* **Build your network:** Whether it’s [your team](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fcreate-and-manage-team), a group of friends, or a community, support each other as you create your goals and then along the way to keep each other accountable. \n* **Plan events and projects:** Got a workshop, challenge, or big event coming up? Staying organized is the key to success. Organize everything from start to finish using [comments](https://hackmd.io/@hackmd-blog/new-commenting-experience) to collaborate. \n* **Share your wins:** There’s nothing like celebrating success. Share your stories and inspire others to start on their own journey.\n\nWhen everyone pitches in and cheers each other on, it’s amazing what we can accomplish. Regular check-ins and shared accountability turn goals into realities.\n\nThe beauty of working as a community is that you don’t have to do it all alone. Lean into the strengths of your group. Collaboration tools can help streamline your efforts—whether you’re brainstorming, documenting ideas, or sharing progress.\n\nFor example, shared documentation tools can organize everything and make it accessible to everyone on the team or throught your community. Use them effectively to outline your plans, assign tasks, and share updates. Focus on achieving the goals and allowing the documentation tool to do the heavy lifting along the way.\n\n### Achieving success together in 2025\n\nAchieving your resolutions isn't always easy, but following these tried-and-true rules can set you up for success:\n1. **Be specific:** Vague goals like \"learn programming\" aren't actionable. Instead, aim for something concrete, like \"complete an online Python course by March.\"\n2. **Write them down:** Putting your goals into writing makes them tangible. Create a document where you can list your resolutions and track your progress.\n3. **Make them measurable:** Define metrics for success. If your goal is to contribute to open-source, set a target of submitting three pull requests by midyear.\n4. **Break goals into smaller steps:** Large goals can feel overwhelming. Break them into smaller, manageable tasks that you can tackle one at a time.\n5. **Set realistic deadlines:** Ambition is great, but overly aggressive timelines can lead to burnout. Create a schedule that pushes you while still being achievable.\n6. **Review progress regularly:** Schedule weekly or monthly check-ins to assess your progress. Use these moments to celebrate wins and adjust your approach if needed.\n7. **Stay accountable:** Share your goals with others who can help keep you on track. Whether it's your team, a mentor, or a community, accountability boosts commitment.\n8. **Celebrate success:** Don't wait until the end of the year to celebrate. Acknowledge milestones as you hit them—every step forward deserves recognition.\n\n### How HackMD can empower teams and communities in 2025\n\nHackMD has many great features that make planning and achieving goals effortless. Here’s how it can help:\n\n* **Collaborating made easy:** Ever tried brainstorming over email chains? Yeah, it’s the worst. With HackMD, everyone can work on the same document at the same time. It’s perfect for bouncing ideas around and organizing things in real-time [edits.](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fsuggest-edit-en)\n* **Keeping track of progress:** Whether it’s checklists, templates, or real-time updates, HackMD helps you monitor progress by sending you email alerts when someone [comments](https://hackmd.io/@hackmd-blog/new-commenting-experience) on your note. \n* **Staying accountable:** Goals stick better when others keep you on track. HackMD makes it easy to check in with teammates, share updates, and motivate everyone.\n\nWhat’s even better? HackMD works seamlessly with tools like [GitHub](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/20/sync-notes-to-github) and [Arweave](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/02/decentralize-documents-hackmd-arweave-integration). That means it fits right into your existing workflows without missing a beat. Everything stays in one neat, tidy place, from assigning tasks to sharing updates.\n\n### A year of growth, achievement, and building in 2025\n\n2025 has so much potential, especially for developers who thrive on collaboration and innovation. By focusing on shared goals and leaning on each other’s strengths, you can make this year one for the books. Let’s roll up our sleeves, set some resolutions, and make them happen—together. \n\nReady to grow? [Join the thousands](https://hackmd.io/home) of people already using HackMD to achieve their goals and build their communities."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"69:T9b6,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe holiday season is officially upon us, and we're here to share a little cheer. ✨ From all of us here at HackMD, we wish you happy holidays and a wonderful New Year!\n\n---\n\n### 'Twas the Night Before the Deadline\n_By HackMD Enthusiasts Everywhere_\n\n'Twas the night before deadline, and all through the teams,\nNot a doc was yet finished—not even the schemes.\nThe drafts were all nestled in workspaces with care,\nIn dreams that revisions soon would be there.\n\nThe users were typing, glued to their screens,\nWhile visions of markdown danced in their dreams.\nWith colleagues in comments and I in my chair,\nWe’d just settled in for a real-time doc share.\n\nWhen out on the workspace arose such a clatter,\nI opened the sidebar to see what’s the matter.\nAway to the changelog I flew like a flash,\nScrolling through updates in one swift markdown dash.\n\nThe UI was sleek, the features a dream,\n“Collaboration perfected!” came a jubilant scream.\nWhen what to my collaborative eyes should appear,\nBut final edits—all crystal-clear!\n\nWith a speedy team leader, so lively and quick,\nI knew in a moment this wasn’t a trick.\nMore rapid than merge requests, changes they came,\nTagged, commented, and called out by name:\n\n“Now bold it! Now italic! Now code-block this text!\nAdd tables! Add headers! What formatting’s next?\nTo the top of the outline! To the end of the wall!\nNow write away, edit away—markdown it all!”\n\nLike stalled ideas before HackMD’s call,\nThey soared in collaboration, no barriers at all.\nSo onto the platform the contributors flew,\nWith brilliant ideas and solutions anew.\n\nAnd then, in a twinkling, I saw on my screen,\nThe power of teamwork—a dazzling scene.\nAs I scrolled down the page, my eyes caught the glow—\nA polished final draft, all ready to go.\n\nThe prose was refined, the structure precise,\nThe kind of result that feels oh-so nice.\nFrom guides to design docs, it all came together,\nProof that collaboration can weather any weather.\n\nNo words were exchanged; we just kept the flow,\nFilling the doc without panic or woe.\nAnd with shortcut keys clicking, the progress was clear,\nA triumph of teamwork and tools we hold dear.\n\nHackMD had saved us—our MVP tool—\nTurning chaos to clarity, simply so cool.\nAnd as the deadline drew closer, a cheer filled the air:\n“Happy hacking to all, and to all a doc share!”\n\n---\n\nAs the year come to an end, we look forward to seeing your projects continue to [grow in 2025.](https://hackmd.io/home)\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6a:T1c70,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n# Master remote teamwork with HackMD during the holidays\nThe holiday season is here, and with it comes all the festive chaos, hot cocoa-fueled brainstorming sessions, and the occasional snowstorm throwing a wrench in your plans. Your team is heads down checking off as much from your to-do list as possible before the holiday break. And it can feel like a lot in a very short amount of time. But don’t worry—[HackMD](https://hackmd.io/home) is like the Rudolph of your workflow, ready to guide your team’s productivity through any storm. Let’s explore how you can sleigh your remote teamwork goals and keep things merry and bright this winter.\n\n## HackMD is built for remote collaboration\nWhether you’re syncing schedules for  holiday projects or crafting next year’s vision, HackMD makes collaborative work easier than ever through remote-friendly tools like commenting, suggest edit, and version history - ideal for remote teams who need to stay connected and productive. \n\nWhen working remote, you can use [the commenting system](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/06/a-new-era-of-commenting) to leave  notes for your teammates  on specific sections of your document or fix typos with the Suggest Edit feature. As your working together, the [version history](https://hackmd.io/s/how-to-save) ensures everyone can see the latest updates or refer back to changes without sending “just checking in” messages, freeing up time for more important things, like cookie decorating contests.\n\nEven better, you can set up task checklists directly within your documents. Assign tasks like “finalize Q4 report” or “design the team’s holiday Zoom background” so everyone knows what’s on their plate—no meetings required.\n\n## Streamline processes for the holidays\n\nThe holiday season is no time to reinvent the sleigh. HackMD documents are setup perfectly for remote teams that work asynchronously. They're your secret weapon for staying organized:\n\n- **Weekly Progress Updates**: Keep it straightforward with sections for wins, priorities, and any roadblocks.\n- **Brainstorming Sessions**: Use Markdown to structure creative sessions, encouraging everyone to add their best ideas for year-end campaigns or festive social posts.\n- **Track Holiday Scheduling**: Keep everyone on the same page with a shared document tracking time-off requests and availability—because no one wants to accidentally schedule a meeting on Christmas day.\n\nThese documents save you time and ensure your team stays on track, so you can focus on spreading holiday cheer instead of wrangling logistics.\n\n## Organize documents with Book Mode\n[Book Mode](https://hackmd.io/s/how-to-create-book) is a standout feature in HackMD that organizes content into structured, easy-to-navigate sections. This is particularly helpful for asynchronous work, as it consolidates:\n\n- Meeting notes in a single, accessible location.\n- Handbooks for ongoing projects.\n- Comprehensive updates and deliverables.\n\nBook Mode provides a clear hierarchy for complex projects, making it easier to locate the information you need. For teams spread across time zones, this ensures everyone has all of the information they need all in one place -- keeping teams aligned and productive, and reducing the need for back-and-forth communication or document hunting. And because of that alignment, it reduces time spent searching for key details and allows everyone to focus on execution. Use Book Mode to document step-by-step processes, ensuring no critical details are missed during task handoffs.\n\n## Flexible sharing options for smooth remote work\nThanks to [customizable permissions](https://hackmd.io/@docs/manage-team-notes), sharing documents on HackMD is effortless. For stakeholders who need updates without editing access, use read-only links. Contributors can be granted editing permissions, enabling them to make updates asynchronously. This flexibility enhances collaboration for remote teams while maintaining control over sensitive information, ensuring smooth workflows.\n\nHackMD’s sharing options can benefit teams working with external collaborators, too. Keep external contributors informed without exposing unrelated or confidential information by granting access only to necessary document sections. This targeted sharing approach enhances efficiency and minimizes the risk of errors.\n\n## HackMD integrations on: The gift that keeps giving\nHackMD is even better when paired with integrations like GitHub and Arweave, enabling you to extend its capabilities for documentation and collaboration:\n\n- [GitHub Integration](https://hackmd.io/s/link-with-github): Sync HackMD documents with GitHub repositories to manage documentation alongside your codebase. Whether you’re drafting README files, updating project wikis, or collaborating on technical documentation, [this integration](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/20/sync-notes-to-github) ensures your work is version-controlled and easily accessible to your development team.\n- [Arweave Integration](https://hackmd.io/@docs/export-import-arweave-en): Use Arweave to archive your HackMD documents on a decentralized network. This ensures your content is [permanently stored and accessible](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/02/decentralize-documents-hackmd-arweave-integration), even if other platforms experience outages. It’s beneficial for preserving critical documentation and maintaining a secure, immutable record of your team’s work.\n\nThese integrations enable you and your team to create a seamless workflow between your documentation, development, and archival needs. Connecting HackMD with GitHub and Arweave will enhance productivity, ensure data integrity, and provide a more comprehensive solution for your team’s collaborative efforts.\n\n## Spread the holiday cheer\nThe holidays are a great time to celebrate your team’s achievements. And those tasks are made even easier for remote teams using HackMD, whose collaborative features allow you to:\n\n- **Highlight significant contributions**. Use comments to highlight excellent ideas or well-executed tasks, reinforcing a culture of appreciation.\n- **Leave thank-you notes or encouraging comments within documents**. A simple acknowledgment can boost morale and show your team that their efforts are valued.\n- **Create a shared holiday message board to foster camaraderie**. Encourage your team members to share their holiday traditions, photos, or messages to bring a personal touch to your workspace.\n\nAcknowledging and appreciating your team’s hard work goes a long way in maintaining morale during the festive season. Celebrations and recognition help build a positive, motivated environment that inspires continued collaboration.\n\n## Sledding into the new year\n\nHackMD is a versatile tool that is the perfect platform for asynchronous collaboration, especially during the holidays. By leveraging its features, setting clear guidelines, and fostering open communication, you can keep your team aligned and productive while enjoying the flexibility you need to balance work with personal commitments now and going into 2025. \n\nStuck in the cold? [Join our community today](https://hackmd.io/home) and see how our tool can help enhance your teamwork during the busy holiday season."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6b:T1730,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nThe season of giving (and markdown!) is here, and we’re back with your festive monthly roundup of fresh updates, visionary voices building on HackMD, and timely industry news.\n\nSo grab a cup of cocoa, cozy up to a warm fireplace, and let’s dive into December’s Markdown Memo! 🎄✨\n\n--\n\n### 🌟 Visionary voices \n- BigSky7 [discusses proximity and Reed-Solomon Codes](https://hackmd.io/IUO7OMrKSQu5Zoigwb6kWQ).\n- Ian-knowable discusses how to [delegate NAM on mainnet](https://hackmd.io/Q1CEGUoeTNG3h_nrIxkXsg?view).\n- PRAGMA-org introduces [Amaru an open-source project](https://hackmd.io/@PRAGMA-org/amaru-proposal) that aims to build a new fully interoperable block-producing node.\n\n### 📄 Product updates \u0026 new features \n#### Update 1.77.3 is now LIVE!\n\nWe’ve been busy elves here at HackMD, working tirelessly to bring you the best editing experience possible! You might notice some exciting new changes as we continue fine-tuning the editor to make it even better for our community. \n\nSome of those updates and changes include:\n\n- Hide comment button when disabled\n- Fix to close the copy menu when switching to view mode\n- Preparing for a NEW feature you all have been asking for 😉 \n\nStay tuned for more surprises—you’re going to love what’s coming next! 🎁\n\n#### Let it snow\nYup! You read that right, this year HackMD is bringing the Christmas spirit to you by turning your workspace into a winter wonderland. Every time you enter one of the trigger words while writing the magic will begin. ❄️\n\nTrigger words include:\n- Let it snow\n- Christmas\n- Holiday\n\nGetting a little too chilly and want to turn off this feature? just click the wrench icon 🔧 at the bottom of your note and uncheck \"Let it snow\" to stop the animation at any time.\n\nMerry Christmas from us here at HackMD! 🎅🏻\n\n**HackMD is available in Japanese**\n\nWe are delighted to announce that the [HackMD website](https://hackmd.io/home?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=nav-bar) is now available in Japanese. We understand the importance of using a tool in your native language, and we are thrilled to offer our Japanese users a more comfortable and intuitive experience.\n\n### 🖋️ From the blog\n#### Enhance your research using HackMD\n\nResearch thrives on collaboration and organization. In fact, those are two research cornerstones: collaborating with others to share knowledge and push innovation forward, while creating organized, repeatable procedures to confirm findings. \n\nWant to know how to make your research better in 2025? [Take a look here](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/11/20/enhance-research-hackmd?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=recent-posts).\n\n#### 2024 Recap\nAs we wrap up an incredible year at HackMD, we’re taking a moment to reflect on 2024 – a year filled with growth and collaboration for you and our team. From launching some of your most-requested features to welcoming more than 160k new teams, creators, and innovators, it’s been an eventful ride. [Read more here](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/12/10/celebrating-2024-hackmds-biggest-moments?utm_source=blog\u0026utm_medium=featured-article).\n\n### 🎁 Holiday Giveaway\nAs promised we have a gift for you this year and it is BIG. We want to give one of YOU a year FREE of HackMD Prime and it is easy to enter. \n\nHere’s how to enter:\n\n- Like and share one of these giveaway posts ([Twitter](https://x.com/hackmdio/status/1866481792661733846), [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/photo?fbid=993961872748735\u0026set=a.451391627005765), or [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/hackmd_the-holiday-season-is-here-and-were-excited-activity-7272247738468024320-J9Yi/?utm_source=share\u0026utm_medium=member_desktop))\n- Follow us on the corresponding channel (i.e. if you liked and shared on Twitter, follow us there)\n- Comment on the post with your HackMD profile link\n\nEveryone has until Friday, January 3rd, 2025 at 11:59pm PT to enter. \nTerms and conditions apply.\n![2024 (1)](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/SkNZlW8NJl.png)\n\n### 📰 Industry news and trends\n- Bitcoin's $100K+ [run is just early days](https://www.coindesk.com/daybook-us/2024/12/04/crypto-daybook-americas-bitcoin-s-usd100k-run-is-just-early-days).\n- AI agents are transforming the [software development life cycle](https://sdtimes.com/ai/ai-agents-are-transforming-the-software-development-life-cycle/).\n- [U.S. crypto stocks surge](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2024/12/05/u-s-crypto-stocks-surge-in-pre-market-trading-as-bitcoin-tops-100-k).\n- Couchbase tackles agentic [AI development challenges](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/couchbase-tackles-agentic-ai-development-challenges/). \n- [Memecoins are here to stay, says expert panel](https://www.theblock.co/post/329649/memecoins-are-here-to-stay-says-expert-panel-but-is-utility-the-goal).\n- Google plans to [merge Chrome OS and Android](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/google-plans-merge-chrome-os-and-android/).\n- Is Ethereum poised to catch up [with Bitcoin and Solana?](https://cointelegraph.com/magazine/ethereum-is-poised-to-catch-up-with-bitcoin-and-solana-in-2025/).\n- Amazon announces [its own series of foundation models, Amazon Nova](https://sdtimes.com/ai/amazon-announces-its-own-series-of-foundation-models-amazon-nova/).\n- Fed Chair Jerome Powell's Hawkish comments [throw some cold water on crypto](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2024/11/14/fed-chair-jerome-powells-hawkish-comments-throw-some-cold-water-on-crypto). \n- GitHub Copilot [users gain access to Stack Overflow knowledge](https://www.developer-tech.com/news/github-copilot-users-access-stack-overflow-knowledge/).\n\nDo you have an article or paper you'd like to feature in the Markdown Memo? We'd love to hear from you. Reply to this email or share it in Discord.\n\n\nAs we wrap up this festive edition of the Markdown Memo, we want to thank you for being part of the HackMD community this year. 🎄\n\nWe can’t wait to see what you build in 2025,\nThe HackMD Team"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6c:T35b8,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nAs we wrap up an incredible year at HackMD, we're taking a moment to reflect on 2024 -- a year filled with growth and collaboration for you and our team. From launching some of your most-requested features to welcoming more than **160k new teams, creators, and innovators**, it's been an eventful ride. And we couldn't have done it without you. 💜\n\nBecause every update and new feature is created together with you -- how you work and what you make fuels us to build a better product. We build and ship, you bring those features to life. \n\nSo we're remembering what we built together, highlighting the features that defined our year, sharing exciting stats about how you've been using HackMD, and giving a sneak peek into our vision for 2025.\n\n\u003e Have you enjoyed using HackMD this past year? Take 5 minutes to fill out the survey in your email inbox. It's insights like yours that help us shape HackMD into a tool you love using.\n\n### Everything we shipped in 2024\n2024 was a busy year for the team. We unveiled updates to enhance your collaboration experience within the text editor -- including a few highly-requested features -- and prioritized accessibility and flexibility.\n\n![HackMD_2024RecaP_ProductsShipped](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/B1W7dkSEke.png)\n\nThe [revamped commenting system](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/06/a-new-era-of-commenting?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) introduced a sleek side panel for better organization, real-time notifications, emoji reactions, and a hybrid mode for grouped discussions, all designed to make teamwork smoother and more intuitive. [Suggest Edit](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/04/17/introducing-suggest-edit?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) was the next to roll out, building off the commenting system and adding a new dimension to collaboration. With Suggest Edit you could now propose changes directly within a note, as well as keep your document tidy by hiding and resolving comments or edits. Along with these enhancements to collaboration, the [floating toolbar and copy link](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/11/07/Copy-Link-Floating-Toolbar?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) features were added, enabling quick formatting and seamless sharing of specific content. \n\nThis year, we prioritized accessibility and flexibility with features like Offline Access, PDF exports, Dark Mode, and OTP support. [Offline Access](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/29/introducing-offline-access?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) ensures your work continues uninterrupted, while enhanced [PDF exports](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/06/12/PDF-exports-OTP-support?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) guarantee polished documents across a range of formats. Alongside PDF exports, we enabled [support for OTP](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/06/12/PDF-exports-OTP-support?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), making account security more user-friendly. And over the summer we unveiled a [native Dark Mode](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/06/27/dark-mode-and-more?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), bringing a sleek and practical look to HackMD.\n\nAll in all, this year we were obsessed with enhancing the text editor and overall product experience so that you can collaborate and innovate with even more efficiency. We're committed to creating a platform that's not just functional, but delightful to use. \n\n### Partnering to bring new web3 capabilities to HackMD\nThis year, we partnered with industry leaders [SpruceID](https://spruceid.com/) and [Login.xyz](https://login.xyz/) to [introduce Sign-In with Ethereum (SIWE) within HackMD](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/26/sign-in-with-eth-is-live?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article). This marks the second major integration on HackMD with [web3 tooling](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/02/decentralize-documents-hackmd-arweave-integration?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article). \n\nThis innovative feature allows users to sign in using their Ethereum wallet and ENS profile, enhancing both security and privacy by eliminating the need for traditional intermediaries. By integrating this authentication method, we’re embracing a future where decentralization meets intuitive workflows, giving our community a seamless yet self-custodial login experience. Whether you're a web3 enthusiast or just value greater control over your data, SIWE marks a bold step forward in redefining how we interact online.\n\n### Company happenings: From rebranding to team growth\nBeyond updates to the product, the HackMD team launched exciting new initiatives, participated in key industry events, and welcomed new team members. \n\n![HackMD_2024Recap_Rebrand](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/SyzN_JSEJx.png)\n\nAfter months of hard work and creativity, we were thrilled to [unveil our company rebrand](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/08/27/HackMDs-bold-new-look?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article). HackMD celebrated its 9th anniversary in November this year and with how much we've changed since then, we realized it was time for our visual identity to grow and evolve with us. We wanted a look that reflects who we are now and where we're headed in the future. \n\nIn 2024, we also [announced a newsletter](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/01/11/hackmd-markdown-memo?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), the HackMD Markdown Memo. In this monthly newsletter, readers learn about communities building with Markdown, new product features, the best ways to harness the platform, and catch up on industry news. If you're not already subscribed, you can sign up to receive the newsletter at the bottom of this page. 👇\n\nWhile we were mainly heads down building, there were times this year when we closed our computers to join you IRL. We kicked off by attending [ETHDenver](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/02/20/see-you-at-ethdenver?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), where we connected with leaders in the blockchain space, and later joined the [Hello World Dev Conference](https://x.com/hackmdio/status/1846200716416692512), immersing ourselves in the vibrant developer community. Our team also took the stage, with [Zoe sharing insights at the Women in Data Science Taipei Conference](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7198336156000825344) and Yukai engaging developers at the Supabase Taipei Meetup. We showed our support for open-source and creativity by sponsoring COSCUP and [NCKU Formula Racing](https://x.com/hackmdio/status/1838282779429138828), reinforcing our commitment to fostering talent and innovation across diverse fields. \n\n\u003ciframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/TVubqRiujxQ?si=7Q3Tp4dgCTyN2L9L\" title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen\u003e\u003c/iframe\u003e\n\nHackMD leaders joined industry podcasts throughout the year. Our [CEO, Jong-Kai Yang](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7177025595061481473), and [Marketing Director, Rachel Golden](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7178477040021499905), joined Genzio Media at ETHDenver to discuss the link between projects and their communities and the importance of documentation. Jong-kai was back on the mic with [Web3 Galaxy Brain](https://x.com/hackmdio/status/1800584786517311875) to highlight the hidden features in the product and why HackMD is rooted in collaboration.\n\nThis year, HackMD welcomed an incredible group of talented individuals to our team, each bringing unique skills and fresh perspectives. From a designer, helping to make our product beautiful, to a custom support specialist, answering all of your questions, to a marketer amplifying our message (you may have seen his name on a few blogs lately 😉), their contributions have been instrumental in shaping HackMD's success in 2024. They've not only strengthened our ability to innovate but have also enriched our culture with their passion and creativity.\n\n### By the numbers\nWe can't stop saying it, but it's true: we couldn't have accomplished everything we did in 2024 without you! From creating, sharing, and collaborating on notes to building connections across teams and countries, HackMD has grown together with you in ways we're beyond proud to celebrate.\n\nHere's a look at some of the amazing things we've accomplished together in 2024:\n\n![HackMD_2024Recap_ByNumbers](https://hackmd.io/_uploads/SkZNiyHVkx.png)\n\nBut it's not just about the numbers, it's about the incredible stories, real people, and communities behind them. One of those communities is SCIST, a group making waves in IT education and showcasing the power of collaboration on HackMD. \n\nSCIST, the Students’ Community of Information in Southern Taiwan, is on a mission to expand IT education among tech enthusiasts in the region. HackMD has been instrumental in [supporting SCIST’s initiatives](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/10/21/SCIST-customer-story?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) by transforming how they collaborate, share knowledge, and connect with their community. From meeting notes to project documentation, the platform enables their team to stay aligned and efficient. They’ve even leveraged HackMD’s “Book Mode” to structure resources and insights, making them accessible for both their team and the wider community. Through HackMD, SCIST continues to connect with like-minded individuals, share knowledge, and inspire other student organizations to embrace collaborative tools.\n\nSCIST is just one example of how HackMD empowers communities, but they’re far from alone. Across industries like [**software development, web3, research, education, startups, and non-profits**](https://hackmd.io/solutions?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=2024recap_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), HackMD is the go-to platform for teams that value collaboration and innovation. HackMD provides the tools to bring people together, spark creativity, and turn ideas into action.\n\n### Our vision for 2025\nOver the past year, we've been heads down redesigning and improving the experience at HackMD. But there's still a lot of work to be done. \n\nAnd as we look ahead to 2025, our vision is centered around one key theme: community. At HackMD, we believe that great things happen when people come together to collaborate, share knowledge, and create. So for this upcoming year, we're not only focusing on how HackMD can help you document ideas but also how it can foster connection and amplify the power of collaboration within your communities. \n\nNext year, we're rolling out improvements to your workspace, including a feature we've been hinting at for a while now. As well as, more updates to make working together in Markdown and sharing your content smoother. We want HackMD to fit even more seamlessly into your existing tech stack so that you can work smarter, not harder. We'll highlight new ways to use HackMD with the tools you already love and maybe partner with a few new ones.\n\nAnd because we're serious about our commitment to fostering community, meeting you IRL will be a stronger focus of ours next year. Whether it's through sponsoring meetups, speaking at events, or just getting out there at the industry events you know and love, we're committed to supporting the communities that make HackMD so special. \n\n2025 is all about bridging gaps -- between ideas and execution, teams and tools, and individuals and communities. \n\n### Celebrating 2024 with a giveaway 🎉\n2024 was a big year for us here at HackMD. And we couldn't have done it without you. 💜 That's why, this holiday season, we're spreading cheer and celebrating everything we've done _together_ with a special giveaway! \n\nOne lucky winner will receive a free year of HackMD Prime, unlocking premium features like unlimited GitHub pushes, unlimited version history, and PDF exports to make building even better. \n\nHere's how to enter:\n1. Like and share one of these giveaway posts ([Twitter](https://x.com/hackmdio/status/1866481792661733846), [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/photo?fbid=993961872748735\u0026set=a.451391627005765), or [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/hackmd_the-holiday-season-is-here-and-were-excited-activity-7272247738468024320-J9Yi?utm_source=share\u0026utm_medium=member_desktop))\n2. Follow us on the corresponding channel (i.e. if you liked and shared on Twitter, follow us there)\n3. Comment on the post with your HackMD profile link\n\nNew users can join in the fun too! Simply sign up for a HackMD account and follow the same steps. Everyone has until **Friday, January 3rd, 2025 at 11:59pm PT to enter**. Terms and conditions apply.\n\nThis giveaway is our way of saying thank you for being a part of the HackMD community and making 2024 so special. There's so much more to come and we can't wait to see what 2025 holds!\n\n\u003e Have you enjoyed using HackMD this past year? Take 5 minutes to fill out the survey in your email inbox. It's insights like yours that help us shape HackMD into a tool you love using."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6d:T279c,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nMarkdown has gained a loyal following among writers, developers, and content creators for its simplicity and flexibility. It enables users to format text using straightforward syntax, making it an excellent choice for those who want to focus on writing rather than wrestling with complex formatting options. \n\nBut while Markdown is simple and straightforward to use, it can be intimidating to choose the right Markdown editor for your needs. That's why we created this beginner's guide. \n\nThis guide will help you understand Markdown, why it's valuable, and how to choose a Markdown editor that aligns with your workflow. We'll also explore some of the top Markdown editors available today and highlight what sets them apart.\n\n## What is Markdown?\n[Markdown is a lightweight markup language](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/04/24/back-to-basics-what-is-markdown?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) that enables you to format plain text documents using simple syntax. Created by John Gruber in 2004, Markdown was designed to be easy to write and read in raw form while versatile enough to convert into HTML or other rich text formats. It was created to be accessible for everyone, regardless of their technical expertise.\n\nAnd it's especially popular among developers and writers who value clean, distraction-free text editing environments. With Markdown, you can format headers, lists, links, images, and more using intuitive commands. For instance, typing # before a line creates a header, while surrounding a word with * or ** adds italics or bold formatting.\n\nOne of Markdown's most significant advantages is its simplicity. Unlike traditional word processors, Markdown doesn't rely on menus or complex formatting options. Instead, it keeps everything in plain text, which makes it lightweight and portable. A Markdown file, saved as .md or .markdown, can be opened and edited in any text editor, ensuring long-term compatibility and accessibility.\n\n## Why use Markdown?\nMarkdown has grown popular across various fields because of its simplicity, versatility, and compatibility. \n\n- **Distraction-free writing**\nFor writers, it offers a distraction-free writing experience with minimal clutter. Developers appreciate its plain text format, which integrates seamlessly with version control systems like Git. Bloggers, technical writers, and educators rely on Markdown to create content that can be easily shared and formatted for web or print.\n\n- **Portability and compatibility**\nMarkdown files are plain text, incredibly portable, and compatible with almost any text editor or operating system. You can write a Markdown file on your laptop and open it on your phone or tablet without losing any formatting. Many modern tools and platforms, such as GitHub, Slack, and documentation software like HackMD, support Markdown natively, making it a universal choice for creating and sharing content.\n\n- **Wide adoption across industries**\nMarkdown has applications across industries, from [developers creating technical documentation](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/10/07/guide-to-creating-technical-design-doc?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) to educators preparing course materials. Its support for advanced syntax, such as code blocks, tables, and mathematical equations, makes it a versatile tool for professionals. \n\nMany Markdown editors provide export options, allowing users to effortlessly convert their Markdown files into HTML, PDF, or Word documents.\n\n## Choosing the right Markdown editor\nNot all Markdown editors are created equal. While some focus on providing a minimalist writing experience, others are designed for collaboration, note-taking, or advanced formatting. When selecting a Markdown editor, consider the following factors:\n- **Ease of use:** The syntax itself is straightforward and intuitive, but how easy is it to use the editor itself? Choosing an editor that prioritizes ease of use ensures a seamless workflow without a steep learning curve.\n- **Live preview:** This lets you see the formatted document alongside the raw Markdown syntax.\n- **Collaboration tools:** Essential for team projects, these tools enable real-time editing and commenting.\n- **Customization:** Options like themes, plugins, and syntax highlighting enhance usability and productivity.\n- **Features provided:** Many editors include advanced functionalities such as support for GitHub Flavored Markdown (GFM), mathematical equations, and [diagrams](https://hackmd.io/@hackmd-blog/what-is-a-uml-diagram?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), which are especially useful for developers and technical writers.\n\nWith these in mind, let's explore some of the most popular Markdown editors and what makes them unique.\n\n## Top Markdown editors for beginners and professionals\n\n### Typora\nTypora is renowned for its What-You-See-Is-What-You-Get (WYSIWYG) interface, which hides the Markdown syntax and displays formatted text directly as you type. This approach offers a simple experience, especially for users who prefer not to see raw Markdown syntax. Typora's clean and distraction-free design makes it a favorite among writers and bloggers.\n\nTypora also offers extensive customization options, including themes and CSS support. It supports advanced features like math equations (LaTeX) and has a built-in file organizer for managing large projects. If you value aesthetics and simplicity, Typora is an excellent choice.\n\n### HackMD\nHackMD is a feature-rich Markdown editor designed for collaboration. It is an excellent choice for teams working on documentation, meeting notes, or collaborative writing projects. HackMD's real-time editing and sharing capabilities make creating, editing, and distributing content among multiple contributors easy.\n\nHackMD supports advanced Markdown syntax, including tables, diagrams, and mathematical equations ([like LaTeX](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/07/demystifying-latex-future-online-mathematics?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article)), making it ideal for technical documentation. Its integration with platforms like [GitHub](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/20/sync-notes-to-github?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), [Arweave](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/02/decentralize-documents-hackmd-arweave-integration?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), Google Drive, and Dropbox enhances workflow efficiency, while its unique Book Mode allows users to organize content into structured formats such as manuals and guides. Whether you're a beginner or an experienced user, HackMD provides an intuitive interface that simplifies the writing process.\n\n### Visual Studio Code (VS Code)\nAlthough primarily a code editor, Visual Studio Code has robust Markdown support through extensions like Markdown All in One. These extensions include live preview, syntax highlighting, and table formatting. Developers often choose VS Code for its integration with Git and other development tools, making it an all-in-one solution for writing Markdown alongside coding projects.\n\n### Obsidian\nObsidian is a Markdown-based note-taking app that emphasizes the connection between ideas. It is designed for users who want to build a \"second brain\" by linking notes and concepts through bidirectional links. Obsidian's knowledge graph feature visualizes these relationships, making it a powerful tool for researchers and academics.\n\nObsidian stores all your data locally, ensuring privacy and security. Its plugin ecosystem allows for extensive customization, enabling users to tailor the app to their needs. Obsidian's versatility makes it a standout choice for advanced users, from task management to academic writing.\n\n## How to get started with Markdown\nWhile it may seem intimidating at first, getting started with Markdown is straightforward, even for beginners. Start by familiarizing yourself with basic syntax like headers, lists, and emphasis. Many editors, including [HackMD, provide built-in tutorials](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2Fs%2Ftutorials?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) or guides to help you learn.\n\nChoose a Markdown editor that aligns with your workflow. Typora or Obsidian might be ideal for individual writing projects, while HackMD is perfect for team collaboration. As you grow more comfortable with Markdown, experiment with advanced features like tables, code blocks, and diagrams to unlock their full potential.\n\nFinally, practice regularly. Consistently using Markdown will become second nature, whether you're taking notes, writing blog posts, or documenting code.\n\n## Why HackMD should be your first choice\n[HackMD](https://hackmd.io/home?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) excels as a beginner-friendly yet powerful tool among the many Markdown editors. Its real-time collaboration features, integration with popular platforms, and support for advanced syntax make it a versatile choice for individuals and teams alike. With HackMD, you can seamlessly transition from simple notes to complex documentation projects.\n\nMarkdown editors have transformed content creation and collaboration, offering a streamlined and efficient approach to writing and organizing information. HackMD stands out as a comprehensive solution, providing intuitive features, seamless integrations, and powerful collaboration capabilities among the available tools. \n\nWhether you're managing technical documentation, team projects, or personal notes, HackMD is designed to enhance productivity and simplify workflows. Take the next step in optimizing your content creation process and [sign up for HackMD](https://hackmd.io/join?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=markdowneditors_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) today."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6e:T253e,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nResearch thrives on collaboration and organization. In fact, those are two research cornerstones: collaborating with others to share knowledge and push innovation forward, while creating organized, repeatable procedures to confirm findings.\n\nYet, when it comes to the day-to-day operations like managing multiple documents, maintaining version control, and collaborating across projects or institutions it can be daunting. [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) simplifies these challenges by offering an all-in-one solution for researchers like you. With its real-time editing, seamless collaboration, and intuitive Markdown-based structure, HackMD transforms how research teams work together.\n\n## Real-time collaboration for writing research papers\nWriting a research paper involves distilling complex information clearly, juggling multiple drafts, and coordinating feedback from co-authors. These processes can become chaotic, especially when using traditional word processors or email to exchange files. We all know the agony of searching for the latest version of a doc or scrolling endlessly through email threads. HackMD eliminates these pain points with real-time collaborative editing.\n\nThe revision history feature allows you to track changes over time and revert to earlier versions if needed, ensuring that everything is recovered. Along with that, HackMD's [commenting](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fcomment-to-collaborate-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) and annotation tools allow you to provide feedback directly within the document, streamlining the review and revision process.\n\n## Organizing research notes, data, and findings\nResearch projects generate a variety of notes, from experimental data to meeting takeaways. HackMD acts as a centralized hub where you can organize these materials. Create individual documents for specific research areas and link them together [using Book Mode](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fcreate-a-book-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) for easy navigation. Whether it's hypotheses, data interpretations, or article summaries, HackMD helps you keep everything organized and easily accessible. Its autosave and cloud-based structure make your work safe and available anytime, anywhere.\n\nFor researchers dealing with complex mathematical equations or formulas, HackMD supports [LaTeX and MathJax](https://hackmd.io/@hackmd-blog/demystifying-latex-future-online-mathematics?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), enabling you to visualize your findings in a precise and professional format. Whether you're creating detailed calculations or presenting statistical results, these tools allow you to embed equations directly into your documents, enhancing clarity and communication.\n\nIn addition to mathematical visualizations, HackMD also supports [UML diagrams](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/21/what-is-a-uml-diagram?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), making it an excellent choice for researchers looking to represent processes, workflows, or data models visually. With UML diagrams, you can map relationships, illustrate systems, and present conceptual overviews that enhance your team's understanding of complex research elements.\n\n## Simplifying collaborative literature reviews\nA thorough literature review often forms the foundation of any research project. Before you get to work, it's crucial to evaluate all the existing research on the topic you're investigating. But this literature review requires input from multiple collaborators, each with unique perspectives. HackMD simplifies this by allowing all contributors to simultaneously work on a shared document.\n\nYou can:\n* Divide the workload by assigning sections or topics to different team members.\n* Use tags and embedded links to organize references and related studies.\n* Highlight key insights or gaps in the literature for group discussion.\n\nHackMD also supports [embedding tables](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fhow-to-create-table-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), charts, and visualizations, making it easier to present trends and findings from your review. This collaborative approach ensures that your literature review is comprehensive and well-organized.\n\n## Turning research notes into polished presentations\nOnce your research is complete, sharing your findings is the next step—whether at conferences, seminars, or lab meetings. HackMD's [slide mode](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/15/turn-research-into-slides?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) transforms your notes into professional-looking slides in just a few clicks.\n\nYou can:\n* Use your existing research notes as a base, saving time and effort.\n* Customize your slides with headings, bullet points, and visuals to match your presentation style.\n* Seamlessly transition between Markdown editing and slide previews to ensure your content is informative and visually appealing.\n\nHackMD's slide export options also make it easy to share your presentations or store them for future reference.\n\n## Preparing documents for publishing\nHackMD simplifies the process of preparing your research for publication. You can export your work to formats like [PDF](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fexport-to-pdf-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) or [HTML](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fexport-and-import-notes-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article), making it easy to meet submission guidelines. HackMD's [integration with GitHub](https://hackmd.io/blog/2023/12/20/sync-notes-to-github?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) allows you to publish directly to repositories for open-source projects. Its Markdown-based structure also works seamlessly with bibliographic tools, streamlining the process of managing citations and references.\n\nFor researchers seeking long-term accessibility and transparency, HackMD's [integration with the Arweave blockchain](https://hackmd.io/blog/2024/05/02/decentralize-documents-hackmd-arweave-integration?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) offers an innovative solution. By publishing your research documents to Arweave, you ensure they are stored permanently in a decentralized and immutable format. This approach is ideal for researchers focused on open science or those looking to preserve their findings for future generations. Arweave guarantees your work remains accessible, providing a unique level of permanence compared to traditional repositories.\n\n## Facilitating collaboration across institutions\nMany research projects involve collaboration with researchers from other institutions, often spanning different time zones and regions. HackMD's cloud-based platform enables seamless collaboration regardless of location.\n\nUse customizable sharing settings to securely share documents with team members. Whether they need to view, comment, or edit, you can grant the appropriate permissions to ensure your work remains secure while encouraging active participation.\n\nHackMD's responsive design means team members can contribute from any device, such as a laptop, tablet, or smartphone. This flexibility is precious for international collaborations, allowing real-time updates and communication.\n\n## Building a knowledge repository for long-term research\nResearch projects sometimes end when the paper is published. But the notes, data, and documents you create during the process form the foundation for future work. HackMD allows you to build a comprehensive and searchable knowledge repository.\n\nOrganize your research materials into folders, link related documents, and [use tags](https://hackmd.io/c/tutorials/%2F%40docs%2Fhow-to-use-tag-list-filter-en?utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) to make retrieval easy. Over time, this repository becomes a valuable resource for your team, supporting ongoing research and inspiring new projects.\n\n## Why HackMD is the perfect tool for academics and researchers\nHackMD combines the simplicity of Markdown with the power of real-time collaboration, making it the ideal platform for researchers like you. Its versatility supports every stage of the research process, from brainstorming ideas during the early stages of your project to drafting papers and presentations for conferences to publishing and archiving your findings for future reference.\n\nBy adopting HackMD, you can save time, enhance collaboration, and produce better research outcomes. Whether working on a solo project or coordinating a large team, HackMD is the tool you've been looking for.\n\nReady to see how HackMD can transform your academic research? [Join our community](https://hackmd.io/join?signup-event=HomeSignupCompleted\u0026utm_source=hackmdblog\u0026utm_medium=blog\u0026utm_campaign=acadmicresearch_blogpost\u0026utm_content=article) and experience the difference for yourself."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6f:T1163,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\nWelcome back to the latest edition of the Markdown Memo. 👋\n\nSettle in, 